796–792 BC

24:2 all the days of Jehoiada the priest The Chronicler clarifies that Joash’s faithfulness lasted only while Jehoiada was alive (vv. 17–18).

24:3 Jehoiada took two wives for him Since most of the house of David had been destroyed by Jehu and Athaliah, Jehoiada needed to ensure that Joash would have descendants to continue David’s royal line (see 22:8–10).

24:4 afterward it was in the heart of Joash Just as the initiative of David and Solomon led to the building of the temple, Joash’s initiative leads to the restoration of the temple.

24:5 But the Levites did not act with haste See note on 2 Kgs 12:4–18.

24:6 the tax of Moses, the servant of Yahweh, and of Most likely refers to the census tax (Exod 30:12–14). In the Kings account there are three different sources of income listed (2 Kgs 12:4).

24:12 And they hired stonemasons and skilled craftsmen Instead of repairing the temple themselves, the priests and Levites hired workers to do it (1 Kgs 12:8).

24:13 to its position The Hebrew word used here, mathkoneth, can be rendered “proper condition” or “original design,” referring to the original plans that came to David from God (see 1 Chr 28:11–19).

24:14 they were offering burnt offerings See note on Ezra 3:3.

24:16 with the kings Jehoiada the priest is given a king’s burial, but Joash the king is not (2 Chron 24:25).

24:18 And they forsook the house of Yahweh Without the guidance of Jehoiada, Joash becomes influenced by the “princes of Judah” and turns to idolatry. The Hebrew word azav is used throughout Chronicles to describe the faithlessness of people (see 12:1–2).

Asherahs and the idols See note on 14:3.

          Asherah DDD

24:20 he will forsake you A fulfillment of David’s warning to Solomon (1 Chr 28:9), as well as Azariah’s prophecy to Asa (2 Chron 15:2).

24:21 stoned him with stones Prophets with unpopular messages were often imprisoned (16:10; 18:23–27). The princes of Judah react even more strongly by killing Zechariah.

in the courtyard of the house of Yahweh Zechariah, Jehoiada’s son, is killed inside the temple. Earlier, Jehoiada had been careful to keep the temple pure, ordering Athaliah to be executed outside the temple (23:14).

24:23 at the turn of the year The same Hebrew phrase is used in 2 Sam 11 to describe the time when kings go out to battle (see note on 2 Sam 11:1).

24:24 Yahweh gave a very large army into their hand A reversal of the normal situation in 1–2 Chronicles. Several kings of Judah achieve victory over large armies by relying on God (2 Chr 13:3–20; 14:9–15). Here, the Syrians defeat the larger army of Judah because of Judah’s faithlessness.

24:25 So they killed him on his bed A fulfillment of Zechariah’s request that God avenge his death (v. 22).

24:27 the scroll of the kings See note on 16:11.



 25:1–28 The Chronicler follows the account of Amaziah’s reign in 2 Kgs 14:1–20, with some additions. Most notably, the Chronicler includes several prophecies not present in 2 Kings. In the first, Amaziah responds positively and is rewarded with victory (2 Chron 25:7–12). After this, however, Amaziah worships foreign gods (v. 14). God sends a prophet to rebuke him, but Amaziah rejects the prophet (vv. 15–16) and is defeated as punishment for his idolatry (vv. 17–22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 0, 0, '25:1–28 The Chronicler follows the account of Amaziah’s reign in 2 Kgs 14:1–20, with some additions. Most notably, the Chronicler includes several prophecies not present in 2 Kings. In the first, Amaziah responds positively and is rewarded with victory (2 Chron 25:7–12). After this, however, Amaziah worships foreign gods (v. 14). God sends a prophet to rebuke him, but Amaziah rejects the prophet (vv. 15–16) and is defeated as punishment for his idolatry (vv. 17–22).


25:2 not with a fully-devoted heart Just like his father, Joash, Amaziah was faithful only for a time (compare 24:2). The account in Kings draws an even closer link between Amaziah and Joash (2 Kgs 14:3).

25:3 who had killed his father the king Joash had been killed by his servants because he ordered the stoning of the prophet Zechariah. See 2 Chr 24:20–26.

25:4 in the law, in the scroll of Moses See 2 Kgs 14:6 and note. Amaziah remains faithful to the Law as he executes justice.

        Justice

each shall be put to death for his own sin Quoted from Deut 24:16.



 25:5–10 The Chronicler includes more details than the 2 Kings account of Amaziah’s battle with the Edomites (2 Kgs 14:7). This story highlights the Chronicler’s theme of relying on God rather than foreign alliances. As with Asa, Amaziah relies on God and is rewarded with victory (see 2 Chron 14:11 and note).


25:5 three hundred thousand chosen for Amaziah’s army is much smaller than the ones amassed by Asa (14:8) and Jehoshaphat (17:14–18). This probably accounts for his decision to hire soldiers from Israel.

25:6 for one hundred talents of silver A talent was about 75 pounds (34 kilograms).

25:7 a man of God A common term for a prophet (1 Sam 2:27; 1 Kgs 13:1; 17:24).

  While the title “a man of God” is common throughout Kings and Samuel, the Chronicler uses it much less frequently. Here, it describes an unnamed prophet; it is used elsewhere to describe Moses (2 Chron 30:16; 1 Chr 23:14), David (2 Chron 8:14), and the prophet Shemaiah (11:2).

          Man of God EDB

Yahweh is not with Israel Part of the reason the prophet tells Amaziah to send the Israelite soldiers home is because of Israel’s apostasy. During the reign of Amaziah, Israel worshiped foreign gods.

25:8 power with God to help and to cause stumbling Another reason the prophet warned Amaziah to send the Israelite soldiers home is because he should rely on God (see note on 14:11).

25:10 they returned to their home in great anger The hired soldiers possibly felt slighted because Amaziah dismissed them. More likely, however, they were upset that they would not have the opportunity to receive any of the spoils of war.

25:11 the Valley of Salt The location of this battleground is uncertain.

Seir The hill country of Edom, southeast of Judah. Seir was originally inhabited by Horites, but they were overtaken by the Edomites (sees Deut 2:12 and note).

          Seir ISBE

          Seir AYBD

25:12 the top of the rock The Hebrew word rendered as “rock” is sela'', which the account in Kings identifies as a specific site (see 2 Kgs 14:7 and note).

25:13 plundered much booty Angry that they were sent home and denied the opportunity to gather war spoils, the hired soldiers from Israel decide to plunder Judah (see 2 Chron 25:10).

25:14 he stood them up to himself as gods Amaziah’s idolatry seems especially egregious considering that God had just given him victory.

25:16 why should you be killed? Amaziah threatens to kill the prophet just as his father, Joash, had done (24:20–21). Kings often responded negatively to prophets who brought an unfavorable message (compare 16:10; 18:25–26).

25:17 Joash The Hebrew text here reads yo’ash (Joash), which is a variant spelling of yeho’ash (Jehoash) used in 2 Kgs 14:8 (see note on 2 Kgs 13:9–13).

let us face one another It is unclear whether Amaziah’s message to Joash (Jehoash) is meant to be friendly.

  It is possible he was looking to join in a marriage alliance like Jehoshaphat did with Ahab (2 Chron 18:1). He also could have been looking to face Joash in battle as a response to the actions of the Israelite soldier’s raids (v. 13). However Amaziah meant his message, Joash interprets it as a sign of arrogance.

25:18 thorn bush See note on 2 Kgs 14:9.

25:23 four hundred cubits Joash (Jehoash) damages about 600 feet of Jerusalem’s city wall. A cubit is about 18 inches in length.

from the Gate of Ephraim The Ephraim Gate is on the north side of Jerusalem. It is most likely the site of the modern Damascus Gate.

          Biblical Places

to the Corner Gate Most likely on the western side of Jerusalem near the modern Jaffa Gate.

25:24 with Obed-Edom Refers to the family that David appointed as gatekeepers.

25:26 the remainder of the words of A common epitaph for deceased kings (compare');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 0, 0, '26:1–23 Uzziah receives far more coverage in 2 Chronicles than in 2 Kings (compare 2 Kgs 14:21–22; 15:1–7). His story shares similarities with those of Joash (2 Chron 23:1–24:27) and Amaziah (25:1–28). All three kings were faithful at the beginning of their reigns but eventually fell away. Their stories would have been a reminder to the Chronicler’s audience of returned exiles to remain faithful.


26:1 Uzziah Also known as Azariah (2 Kgs 15:1).

          Uzziah EDB

          Uzziah HIBD



  Uzziah
 Uzziah was an early eighth-century BC king of Judah. His name means “Yahweh is (my) strength.” Uzziah enjoyed a long reign, and the kingdom of Judah experienced its greatest prosperity since Solomon. Uzziah improved the defenses of Judah and expanded its territory through a series of military successes. According to 2 Kings and 2 Chronicles, Yahweh struck Uzziah with leprosy. The disease forced Uzziah to hand over control of the kingdom to his son, Jotham, while he spent the remainder of his life in isolation.


26:2 He built Elath A site on the Gulf of Aqaba (see note on 2 Kgs 14:22).

slept with his ancestors See note on 2 Chr 9:31.

26:3 he reigned in Jerusalem fifty-two years From approximately 792–742 BC. Uzziah’s reign most likely included periods of coregency. He apparently ruled when his father, Amaziah, fled to Lachish (25:27–28; 26:1). Uzziah’s son, Jotham, served as coregent after Uzziah contracted leprosy (v. 21).




Northern King
Date
Southern King
Date
Jehoash
798–786 BC
Amaziah
796–767 BC
Jeroboam II
786–747 BC
Uzziah (Azariah)
792–742 BC
