INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 16, 3, 'a covenant This treaty is not mentioned elsewhere. It might be the reason Jeroboam did not become powerful during Abijah’s reign (13:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 16, 4, 'against the cities of Israel The cities named here are in the Upper Galilee region.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 16, 6, 'Geba and Mizpah Strategic cities north of Jerusalem. See 1 Kgs 15:22 and note.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 16, 7, 'Hanani the seer Mentioned only here in the Bible. Hanani’s son, Jehu, prophesied to Baasha (1 Kgs 16:1) as well as Asa’s son, Jehoshaphat (2 Chron 19:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 16, 8, 'a mighty army with very abundant See 14:9 and note.

because of your reliance on Yahweh See note on 14:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 16, 9, 'from now on you will have wars Earlier, Asa was given peace when he sought God (14:6–7; 15:19). Now that he has stopped relying on God and has started relying on foreign alliances, he will face conflict.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 16, 10, 'was angry with the seer Asa’s reaction is in line with later kings who oppressed prophets who brought unfavorable messages (18:12–27; 24:20–21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 16, 11, 'the words of Asa from the first to the last A common epitaph for deceased kings (1 Kgs 11:41; 14:19).

the scroll of the kings of Judah and Israel The Chronicler refers to this source several times (e.g., 2 Chr 27:7; 35:27). The exact nature of the source is unknown, but it most likely is not the same work as the biblical 1–2 Kings. Second Chronicles refers to this source in contexts for which 1–2 Kings gives no further information. Compare 27:1–9; 2 Kgs 15:32–38.

          The Chronicler’s Sources 1, 2 Chronicles (NAC)

          Sources EBC 1Ki—Job');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 16, 12, 'he fell severely ill in his feet See 1 Kgs 15:23 and note.

he did not seek Yahweh The Chronicler notes that Asa ended his reign by not seeking God even when diseased. Rather than relying on God, he relied on physicians. See 2 Chron 14:4 and note; 14:11 and note.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 16, 13, 'slept with his ancestors See note on 9:31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 16, 14, 'all kinds of spices made by the perfumers as a fragment ointment A sign of honor (compare Jer 34:5). Even though Asa’s story ends with several failings, the honor he received in death corresponds with the positive assessment of him in 2 Chron 14:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 17, 1, 'Jehoshaphat receives extensive coverage in 2 Chronicles. He is portrayed in a mostly positive light (vv. 3–4; 22:9), but he also is criticized for his alliances with the northern kings Ahab and Ahaziah (19:2; 20:37). The Chronicler focuses on Jehoshaphat’s reforms, which include sending officials throughout the cities of Judah to teach the people (vv. 7–9) and appointing judges to administer justice throughout Judah (19:5–11). By including the criticism of Jehoshaphat’s alliances, the Chronicler emphasizes reliance on God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 17, 1, 'Jehoshaphat receives extensive coverage in 2 Chronicles. He is portrayed in a mostly positive light (vv. 3–4; 22:9), but he also is criticized for his alliances with the northern kings Ahab and Ahaziah (19:2; 20:37). The Chronicler focuses on Jehoshaphat’s reforms, which include sending officials throughout the cities of Judah to teach the people (vv. 7–9) and appointing judges to administer justice throughout Judah (19:5–11). By including the criticism of Jehoshaphat’s alliances, the Chronicler emphasizes reliance on God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', '2 Crônicas', 17, 1, 'reigned in his place Jehoshaphat reigned for 25 years (20:31; 1 Kgs 22:42).




