INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Jó', 0, 0, '21:1–34 Each of Job’s friends has argued that the wicked suffer (15:17–35; 18:5–21; 20:4–29). Here, Job responds by claiming that the wicked actually prosper. He opens by addressing his friends—asking them to listen and understand his complaint (vv. 2–6). He then argues that the wicked live long and prosperous lives (vv. 7–16) and that they are not punished (vv. 17–26). Job anticipates his friends’ response and argues that travelers could support his claims (vv. 27–33). He concludes by rebuking his friends’ comfort (v. 34).


21:2 let this be your consolation Job asked his friends to pay attention to his words. Since they failed as comforters (16:2), Job tells them to be comforted by their silence and attention (13:8).

21:3 then after my speaking you can mock Job holds no real hope for understanding from his friends. He sarcastically requests that they wait for him to finish speaking before they resume mocking him.

21:4 my complaint for human beings Job’s main complaint is not against his friends; his real grievance is with God, who has afflicted him for no apparent reason (see 9:17; 16:7).

21:5 be appalled Job wishes his friends would react in horror at his unjust treatment. See note on 17:8.

place your hand on your mouth An expression showing someone is silent or speechless (29:9; 40:4).

21:7 grow old, even grow mighty in power Job contradicts his friends by claiming that the wicked lead long and productive lives (20:5; compare 4:7 and note; 4:8 and note).

          Retribution EDB

          Retribution ISBE

21:8 their offspring are established Job’s friends claimed that the wicked had no posterity (18:19; 20:21). Here, Job argues that the wicked live to see their descendants prosper.

21:9 are safe without fear Contradicts Eliphaz’s claims that the wicked are surrounded by fearful sounds (15:21).

the rod of God is not upon them Job complained that God’s rod was upon him (9:34).

21:11 their children dance around The children of the wicked are safe and carefree—a direct contrast to how Zophar characterizes them (20:10).

21:13 They spend their days in prosperity Job argues that the wicked live prosperous lives. This contradicts the claims of Job’s friends that the wicked do not enjoy peaceful or prosperous lives, but undergo quick and dramatic destruction (15:20, 29; 18:18–20; 20:7–11).

        Old Testament Theology of the Afterlife



Sheol The Hebrew text here uses the term she’ol, a reference to the realm of the dead. See note on 14:13.

21:14 they say to God Eliphaz directly attributed the suffering of the wicked to their defiant attitude toward God (15:25). Job describes that same attitude here, but argues that they are not punished for it.

21:16 their prosperity is not in their hands Ironically, even though the wicked defiantly reject God and boast that worshiping Him gains them nothing (v. 15), God is still responsible for their prosperity (10:3).

21:17 How often Job responds to Bildad’s claims that the light of the wicked is put out (18:5–6). He challenges Bildad by asking how often this occurs.

their disaster See 18:12.

in his anger See 4:9 and 20:23.

21:19 stores up his iniquity for his children Job seems to be quoting an opinion of his friends or someone like them—for this reason, many translations supply the words “you say” or “they say” (compare 5:4; 20:10). He argues that the wicked do not care what happens after they are gone (v. 21), and that they should personally face God’s judgment (vv. 19–20).

          Proverb Unlike Job Job (NAC)

21:26 They lie down together in the dust Job cites examples of two different scenarios: One person dies after living a rich and secure life (v. 23), another dies after living a difficult and poor life (v. 25). Ultimately, both die. Any prosperity enjoyed by the first person does not carry beyond death. The author of Ecclesiastes also makes this point (see Eccl 2:15–17; 6:6; 9:2).

          Job’s Objections to Retribution TOTC Job

