 14:1–9 Hosea concludes with a call for Israel to repent and a promise of Yahweh’s forgiveness and future restoration.


14:2 the fruit of our lips Probably refers to Israel’s verbal confession, which is more important than actual sacrifices (compare Psa 50:7–23).

14:3 Assyria will not save us Israel’s confession includes an acknowledgement of its two main failings: seeking foreign political aid and serving manmade idols. The confession also alludes to past injustice by mentioning Yahweh’s concern for the orphan (Exod 22:22–23).

14:4 I will heal their disloyalty Yahweh responds to Israel’s confession. He promises to heal the people, reassuring them of His love and the temporary nature of His wrath.

14:5 he will blossom like the lily plant Oracles of salvation regularly employ imagery of renewed fertility and natural abundance. Compare Isa 27:6.

Lebanon See note on Isa 2:13.

14:8 a luxuriant cypress The prophet compares Yahweh to a tree, referring to its ability to provide fruit. This imagery follows several other plant metaphors in Hos 14:5–7 for both God and Israel. The tree metaphor also connects to the protective shade offered in v. 7.

