INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 0, 0, '1:1 Habakkuk is set in the southern kingdom of Judah during the late seventh century BC—a few decades before the Babylonians destroyed Jerusalem and deported the people of Judah.
   The book begins with the prophet’s complaint to Yahweh over the injustices in Judah that He seemingly permits (Hab 1:1–4). Yahweh sends His answer: the Chaldeans (another name for the Babylonians; see note on v. 6) will be His instrument of discipline (vv. 5–11). Habakkuk wonders how God can use a wicked nation to carry out His plans (1:12–2:1). Yahweh answers by chastising the prophet (2:1–3), declaring that the righteous will live by faith (2:4). God then announces the destruction of the Chaldeans in the form of five woe pronouncements (2:6–20). Yahweh closes the section by demanding that “the earth be silent before Him” (2:20). The prophet, moved to worship and praise God, concludes the book with a psalm of adoration and declaration of faith (3:1–19).


        Introduction to Habakkuk

        The Minor Prophets

        How to Study the Bible



oracle See note on Nah 1:1.

        Prophetic Commissioning and the Divine Presence

Habakkuk A prophet of Judah who ministered prior to the exile. Habakkuk was a contemporary of Zephaniah and Jeremiah. Habakkuk’s name seems to be derived from the Hebrew word chabaq, meaning “embrace,” and may indicate that he wrestled with God.

          Habakkuk HIBD

          Habakkuk EBD

          Habakkuk, the Book Of HBD



 1:2–4 Habakkuk complains that wicked leaders are oppressing the righteous remnant within Judah. According to the formula Yahweh established, the wicked are supposed to suffer while the righteous prosper (compare Lev 26; Deut 28). In Habakkuk’s day, this formula seems to have been reversed.


1:2 how long shall I cry for help and you will not listen? How long The prophet has been voicing his concern to Yahweh for quite some time.

you will not listen Since Yahweh had not yet responded to the prophet’s prayers, Habakkuk wondered whether God even heard his supplications. The prophet undoubtedly assumed that if Yahweh had heard his prayers, He would have answered.

Violence Several other prophets bemoaned the presence of violence among the Israelites (see Jer 6:7; Ezek 7:23; Amos 3:10; Mic 6:12; Zeph 1:9).

1:3 Why do you cause me to see evil Habakkuk was forced to witness violence and wickedness on a continual basis. These sins may have been committed by wicked leaders in Judah. Josiah certainly had many counselors and magistrates ruling the nation on his behalf—assuredly the same ones who served his father and grandfather during their oppressive reigns (see note on Hab 1:1). This situation was unbearable for the righteous prophet. He used six different words in vv. 3–4 to describe the social climate: “iniquity,” “destruction,” “violence,” “strife,” “contention,” and “wickedness.” Because of the moral corruption, the law was paralyzed and justice perverted.

  It is possible that the reference here is to a foreign nation that is oppressing Judah. The first suggestion is the Assyrians, who tormented the Jews during Sennacherib’s invasion in 701 BC. Another possibility is the Egyptians, who afflicted the Jews during the invasion of Pharaoh Neco in 609 BC. A final suggestion is the Babylonians, who eventually conquered Jerusalem in 586 BC, destroying the city and exiling its inhabitants.

          Biblical Places

contention The Hebrew word used here, riv, refers to disputes and quarrels between people, and is often used in the Prophets to denote Yahweh’s covenantal lawsuit against those guilty of breaking His laws (see Isa 3:13; Jer 2:9; Hos 4:1).

          Riv WSOTDICT

1:4 the law is paralyzed The Hebrew verb used here denotes that the Mosaic law has grown numb. The same word is used of Jacob’s stunned reaction to the news that his son Joseph was still alive (Gen 45:26). Here, it refers to the law being rendered powerless and ineffective.

justice does not go forth perpetually The rulers of Israel were responsible for ensuring justice for all people in the land (see Mic 3:1).

          Mishpat TWOT

the wicked surround the righteous The oppressors were apparently located within Judah.



 1:5–11 On this occasion, Yahweh chooses to answer Habakkuk’s repeated prayer (compare Hab 1:2).


1:5 in your days The Babylonians likely first arrived in Jerusalem in 605 BC. However, the news of the rise of the Babylonians would have reached Israel shortly after their conquest of Nineveh in 612 BC.

that you will not believe The news of the rise of the Chaldeans (or Babylonians) would have come as an absolute shock to the prophet. At the time of Habakkuk, Assyria dominated the ancient Near East. The Assyrian Empire was experiencing its golden age led by Ashurbanipal, one of the empire’s most successful kings.

1:6 look! I am raising up the Chaldeans Yahweh promised to raise up a nation that would be used to punish the wicked leaders of Israel. The idea that God would raise up foreign armies to punish the Jews for their sins is a common theme in prophetic literature.

        The Babylonian Empire

the Chaldeans The OT writers often used “Chaldeans” and “Babylonians”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 0, 0, '2:1 my post Watchmen were stationed at the highest part of a city wall to warn of impending danger (see Ezek 33:2–6). As watchmen, prophets were to communicate the divine message to the people (see Jer 6:17; Ezek 3:17–21; Hos 9:8).

my complaint The Hebrew term here can be used in the sense of argument (see Psa 38:14) or rebuke (see Psa 39:11).

2:2 Write the vision The vision might be a reference to the revelation concerning the imminent Chaldean invasion described in Hab 1:5–11. It is also possible the vision consists of the exhortation of vv. 3–5, an appeal for patient endurance marked by faithfulness. A final possibility is the revelation of Yahweh’s appearance in 3:3–15 (compare Nah 1:1 and note).

make it plain Perhaps indicating that the writing should be large and easy to read quickly.

on the tablet Clay tablets, though stone tablets are possible. These tablets would have been placed in public gathering spots. For a later example of this, see the deuterocanonical writing 1 Maccabees 14:25–49.

it might be read quickly A reference generally to the inhabitants of Judah, who would flee after reading the announcement of the coming invasion. Otherwise, it may more specifically refer to messengers spreading the news.

2:3 it will give witness to the end The fulfillment of the vision is constantly drawing closer.

wait for it The fulfillment of the prophecy of Hab 1:5–11 would take at least 20 years, depending on the date of Habakkuk’s ministry. During this wait, many scoffers would arise, questioning the authenticity of the prophet’s vision. Yahweh’s encouragement is to wait in patience and faith.

2:4 within him is puffed up; it is not upright The soul of the haughty Babylonians is not right in God’s eyes. They will soon experience divine judgment (see vv. 5–20).

the righteous shall live by his faithfulness Righteous people are commanded to live by faith; they must practice obedience and trust that Yahweh will remain faithful to His covenantal promises.

  The New Testament cites v. 4 three times. For the author of Hebrews, who seems to be drawing on an ancient Greek reading of the passage from the Septuagint, vv. 3–4 provides OT support for the call to patiently await the fulfillment of God’s promise rather than sliding back in faith (see Heb 10:38). Paul twice cites Hab 2:4 in his letters to explain God’s gift of righteousness on the basis of faith (Rom 1:17; Gal 3:11). Paul finds in Hab 2:4 a scriptural citation that justification is given by God in His faithfulness to all people—both Jews and non-Jews—who place faith in His Son.

        Faith

2:5 his throat The Babylonians’ greed is as insatiable as the appetite of death.

Sheol Used throughout the OT to identify the place of the dead (see Gen 37:35; Num 16:33; 1 Sam 2:6; 1 Kgs 2:6; Job 21:13; Psa 6:5; Prov 5:5; Isa 28:15; Ezek 31:17; Hos 13:14).

          Sheol HIBD

        Old Testament Theology of the Afterlife



 2:6–20 The woe pronouncements of Hab 2:6–20 are neatly structured into five stanzas consisting of three verses each. Verses 6–8 denounce the Chaldeans’ imperialistic avarice; vv. 9–11 condemn the Chaldeans’ covetousness; vv. 12–14 criticize the Chaldeans’ violence; vv. 15–17 censure the Chaldeans’ treachery; and vv. 18–20 address the Chaldeans’ idolatry. While the first four woes deal with the Chaldeans’ sins against their fellow humans, the fifth addresses the Chaldeans’ sin against God. The first and fourth woes end in a refrain, possibly a chorus sung by a congregation of people.


2:6 a taunt The Hebrew word used here, mashal, is the common term for a proverb. The taunt song was likely used as a derogatory proverbial chant against an object of scorn (see Deut 28:37; 1 Sam 10:12; Isa 14:4; Jer 24:9; Mic 2:4). See note on Prov 1:1.

  The taunts sung by the righteous are full of mockeries and insinuations. They laugh in the face of the enemy, trusting God to repay their evil deeds. Even though Yahweh allowed His people to be punished by the evil, rapacious Babylonians, the conduct of the oppressor offended God and brought His judgment. The Jews were well aware that the conduct of the Chaldeans would be punished by God; if Yahweh had punished His chosen people for doing deeds contrary to His laws, surely He would exact judgment on the pagan nations that did the same.

          Mashal TWOT

all of these The righteous of Hab 2:4 or the beleaguered nations of v. 5.

Woe to him who heaps up what is not his’? For how long? And, ‘Woe to The Hebrew word used here, hoy, denotes a divine threat. In this form, the prophetic oracle is called a “woe pronouncement.”

          Hoy TWOT

For how long A mournful cry by the oppressed nations, the righteous remnant, or Habakkuk himself (see 1:2).

him who makes himself heavy with pledges Pledges are items used as security in case of default on a loan. They were probably confiscated prematurely and without thought for the needs of the borrowers. This practice could lead to the enslavement of the borrowers (see Neh 5:1–5) because the last pledge or surety they could offer would be themselves. The practice of usury was forbidden by the Mosaic law (see Deut 24:10; compare Job 24:3, 9–10).

          Loan HBD

2:7 your creditors Refers to those nations that suffered oppression at the hands of the Babylonians.

you shall be as plunder for them A reversal of fortune reminiscent of Obad 15.

  The conquests of the Babylonians are amply documented. Their reputation as plunderers was well known even before they became a world power, and they built their empire with bloodshed and violence (see Hab 1:9; 2:12, 17). Verse 7 explains that the nations that had been plundered by the Babylonians would have their revenge. Because of their greed, the Babylonians acquired debts to the nations they ravished. The debt would have to be repaid, and the urgency of calling in the debt predicted here quickly came to pass. The Babylonian Empire lasted less than 100 years (626–539 BC), coming to an abrupt end less than 25 years after the death of Nebuchadnezzar at the hands of the Medes and the Persians under Cyrus. Daniel reports that while Belshazzar held a feast for his noblemen, the Medes and the Persians conquered the great city Babylon (see Dan 5). For thousands of years the ruins of Babylon lay desolate, a reminder to all nations of Yahweh’s vindictive justice.

    

2:9 him who obtains profit from evil gain for his house The Chaldean empire’s thirs');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 0, 0, '3:1–16 Habakkuk’s prayer is in the form of a psalm designed to be sung (see Hab 3:19). Yahweh’s answer filled the proud prophet with fear and awe (see 2:2–20). Habakkuk humbly responded with prayer and praise. The psalm here is filled with exodus imagery, as the prophet vividly recounts the theophany on Mount Sinai, the wilderness wanderings, and the crossings of the Red Sea and Jordan River.


        Theophany in the Old Testament

3:1 according to Shigionoth The Hebrew word used here refers to the musical setting of the psalm, either the melody or style in which it was to be sung. See Psa 7:title.

3:2 to show compassion The term here carries the deeper sense of compassion.

3:3 from Teman One of the principal cities of Edom. The name comes from Teman, the grandson of Esau (see Gen 36:11). The city of Teman can be identified with the modern Tawilan, located 50 miles south of the Dead Sea near Petra. In Obadiah 9, Teman is used for the entire nation of Edom, though this is most likely a case of synecdoche.

          The Biblical World

          Teman EDB

Mount Paran A wilderness region just to the south of the border of Canaan. The Israelites camped at Paran after leaving Mount Sinai. The spies were sent into the promised land from Paran (see Num 13–14).

          Paran HBD

  Selah A musical notation indicating a time of silence for reflection (see Hab 3:9, 13). Certain translations, such as the NIV, do not include this term in the text.

3:6 the mountains of old were shattered The mountains quaking at the presence of Yahweh is a common motif in the OT (see Judg 5:4–5; Psa 114:4–7; Jer 4:24; Nah 1:5).

3:7 Cushan A nomadic tribal group that wandered the deserts of Arabia.

          Cushan HIBD

the land of Midian trembled As the Israelites journeyed through their territory, the Cushanites and Midianites feared for their safety. The land of Midian was in the northwestern part of the Arabian Peninsula.

3:8 against the rivers The Red Sea and Jordan River, both of which the Israelites crossed on dry ground.

chariot A vehicle of two wheels, usually pulled by two horses. Chariots were powerful instruments of war that could dominate battles. In effect, they were the tanks of ancient warfare.

          Chariots HIBD

3:9 your bow Military weapons that could shoot arrows a distance of hundreds of yards. Archers were of such great significance in ancient battles that the Hebrew term for “bow” was often used figuratively for warfare (see Isa 22:3).

          Bow and Arrow EDB

3:10 a torrent of waters A reference to the Jordan River, which was at the flood stage when the Israelites crossed into the promised land (see Josh 3:15).

3:11 Sun and moon stood still Possibly an allusion to the sun standing still in Josh 10:12–13.

3:12 in anger you trampled the nations A threshing floor is a site where sheaves of harvested grain were brought to have the grain dislodged from the stalk. Threshing was done either by using a flail or a threshing sledge. A somewhat violent process, this is an apt illustration of Yahweh’s judgment of the nations.

          Threshing Floor EDB



3:13 anointed The Hebrew term mashiach (meaning “anointed one”) is probably a reference to Moses, although Joshua is another possibility. Throughout the OT, it is used to designate individuals specially chosen by God to perform a divine service, including David (2 Sam 22:51), Saul (1 Sam 24:6), and even the Persian king, Cyrus (Isa 45:1).

          Mashiach TWOT

3:14 You pierced the head of his warriors with his own arrows Perhaps a reference to the armies of Sihon and Og (see Num 21:21–25, 33–35).

3:16 the people attacking us Refers to the Babylonians (Chaldeans). See note on Hab 1:6.

3:17 the yield of the olive tree In this verse, figs, grapes, and olives represent the entire agricultural system of Israel. Olive trees are slow-growing evergreens that produce an oil-bearing fruit. Olive trees are ubiquitous in Israel, and the fruit or its oil is consumed at virtually every meal.

          Olive EDB

          Fig HBD








Zephaniah');
