INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Ageu', 2, 9, 'and in this place I will give peace The Hebrew word used here, shalom, meaning');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, 0, '1:1–8:23 Zechariah is set after the Jewish people had experienced exile in Babylon following the desolation and complete takeover of their land by foreign powers. Zechariah prophesied to a small community of Jews living among the ruins of Jerusalem after returning from exile in Babylon. This community had begun rebuilding the temple on their initial return around 537 bc (Ezra 3:10–12), but the work had stopped when the Jews faced opposition from their neighbors (Ezra 4:4–5). The prophets Haggai and Zechariah encouraged the community to begin rebuilding again (Ezra 5:1–2; Zech 4:9).
     •      The book of Zechariah is commonly divided into two major sections—chs. 1–8 and chs. 9–14. This first section is dominated by a series of eight visions, culminating in the crowning of Joshua, the high priest (6:9–15). The section concludes with a series of four messages addressing proper worship, reflecting on Israel’s past failures, and looking ahead to divine restoration (7:1–8:23). Apocalyptic Literature
           Structure and Themes EBCV8:DMRE


        Introduction to Zechariah

        The Minor Prophets

        How to Study the Bible



1:1 eighth month Corresponding to late October and early November. Zechariah’s ministry began about a month before Haggai received his final vision (see Hag 2:10, 20).

        Israelite Calendar Table

the second year of Darius 520 BC. Darius I Hystaspes ruled Persia from 522–486 BC (see note on Hag 1:1). The visions of Zech 1–6 date to the same period as the events in Ezra 4:24–6:13.

          Darius HIBD

the word of Yahweh A standard introductory formula used by the prophets to indicate that they were communicating God’s words, not their own (e.g., Hos 1:1; Joel 1:1; Mic 1:1; Hag 1:1; Zeph 1:1; see note on Isa 1:10).

        Prophetic Commissioning and the Divine Presence

the prophet A prophet is a spokesperson for God (see note on Mic 3:5).

Zechariah A member of one of the priestly families of Judah. His name means “Yahweh remembers.” His grandfather Iddo had returned from the exile with Zerubbabel (Neh 12:1–7). Zechariah eventually became the head of the priestly clan of Iddo (Neh 12:16).

  Although Zechariah was the son of Berechiah (also rendered “Berekiah”) and the grandson of Iddo (Zech 1:1, 7), the book of Ezra indicates he was a descendant of Iddo (Ezra 5:1; 6:14)—likely to indicate that he was a member of that priestly family. Zechariah probably was a young man when he began to prophesy; the Hebrew term used to identify him in Zech 2:4 typically means “boy” or “youth.” Like Jeremiah and Ezekiel, Zechariah was both a prophet and a priest. The writing prophet (and priest) Zechariah must be distinguished from the priest Zechariah (also the son of Berechiah), who was killed by King Joash around 800 BC (2 Chr 24:20–22; compare note on Matt 23:35). Nothing is known of Zechariah’s fate. It is possible that Zech 9–14 was prompted by the unsuccessful invasion of Greece by the Persians in 481–480 BC. If that is the case, the prophet lived at least until this date.

          Zechariah (Person) AYBD

          Zechariah ZEB Q—Z

1:2 your ancestors Refers to the pre-exilic generations whose sin against Yahweh brought on His judgment and led to the destruction of Jerusalem and the temple.

1:3 Yahweh of hosts The title emphasizes Yahweh’s role as commander-in-chief of the heavenly armies, evoking the metaphor of Yahweh as a warrior (see 1 Sam 17:45).

Return The Hebrew term used here conveys the idea of repentance (e.g., Jer 25:5; 35:15; Mal 3:7).

  This command to repent, placed at the beginning of Zechariah, functions as a call to the people to turn to Yahweh and demonstrate their commitment to Him by rebuilding the temple (see Jer 15:19 and note).

          Repentance LTW

          Shub WSOTDICT

        Repentance

1:4 the former prophets Refers to the pre-exilic prophets, whose warnings of impending judgment and calls to repentance were routinely ignored.

Return from your evil ways A general description of the words of the prophets (see Isa 31:6; Jer 3:22; Ezek 18:30). Zechariah probably isn’t referring to a specific prophet, but the call to return evokes the essence of Jeremiah’s preaching (see Jer 15:19; 18:11; 25:5; 35:15).

        The Significance of “Shuv” in Jeremiah

1:5 Your ancestors, where are they A rhetorical question indicating that the nation’s ancestors had long since passed away.

the prophets, do they live forever This rhetorical question demonstrates that the former prophets have died.

1:6 my words and my regulations The total body of divine revelation. Refers to the words spoken by prophets and the Law.

And they repented The survivors who had gone into exile repented after experiencing judgment (see Jer 31:18–19).

so he has dealt with us The remnant acknowledged that they deserved their judgment.



 1:7–6:8 This passage records eight visions that appear to be in a rough chronological sequence stretching from the time of writing to the coming of the Messiah. The overriding theme of the visions is the expectation of the coming Messiah. This first vision seems to parallel the eighth vision (Zech 6:1–8), as both feature four kinds of horses. The remaining visions also seem to be structured in parallel, in order to present the oracle about the messianic Branch (3:8–10) as the central point of chs. 1–6. The first vision in vv. 7–17 is a pronouncement of hope for the nation. Yahweh promised to put an end to Jerusalem’s 70 years of misery and return the people to the city with comfort and compassion.
   Zechariah may have experienced all eight visions in a single night, but the text never explicitly indicates this. Since a date is given only at v. 7—and because the vision reports are presented in a continuous chain linked by the formula “I lifted my eyes and saw”—it can be inferred that Zechariah saw one vision after another in close successi');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, 0, '2:1–13 The third vision reveals the importance of Jerusalem in God’s restoration program for Israel. Rebuilding the city was a necessary part of the restoration, but this vision points ahead to the ultimate fulfillment of a city protected by Yahweh’s presence (v. 5; compare Rev 21:1–4), not physical walls. The Babylonians’ destruction of the city and temple was caused not by the failure of Jerusalem’s physical defenses, but by the withdrawal of Yahweh’s protective presence (compare Ezek 10:18–19).
   Rebuilding the temple will make way for Yahweh’s presence to return because Yahweh desires a holy place to dwell. The vision addresses the conflicting priorities of the returned exiles: rebuild the temple, or rebuild the city walls in order to protect the temple. Yahweh’s messenger is informing them of the proper priority—rebuilding the temple.
           Zechariah 2:1–13 TOTC HagZecMal


2:1 a man, and in his hand was a measuring rope This figure with a measuring line is likely in the background of Rev 11:1, which draws on imagery from Zechariah (see Zech 1:16) and Ezekiel (see Ezek 40:3 and note).

2:2 what is its length The man measures Jerusalem to prepare to build a wall around the city. He seems to be planning to measure Jerusalem’s former size to rebuild the ruined wall, unaware of Yahweh’s plan for an expanded Jerusalem.

2:4 Run The second angel dispatches the first to reveal to the young man that his plan is not necessary for God’s purpose.

young man This Hebrew term typically “boy” or “youth.” The characterization may serve to emphasize his youthful ignorance; he has not yet learned that Yahweh’s plans are not limited by human perceptions of what is possible.

like villages without walls The population of Jerusalem will be so great that it cannot be contained by a walled city. An unwalled city also symbolized openness to all who would come (see Zech 8:20–22; compare Ezek 38:11).

  The image points to a future fulfillment when all Israel and all the nations are gathered under Yahweh’s rule, a prominent theme throughout the Prophets. Compare Zech 2:11; Isa 2:2–3; 11:12; 43:5–6; 49:16–21; 54:2–3; 60:3–14; 66:18; Jer 3:17; 23:3; 29:14; 31:8–11; 32:37; Ezek 11:17; 28:25–26; 34:13–14; Hos 1:11; Mic 2:12–13.

          Zechariah 2:4 TOTC HagZecMal

2:5 a wall of fire all around An unwalled city was vulnerable to attack (Ezek 38:11). This lack of visible, physical protection likely motivated the exiles (represented by the man with the measuring line) to make preparations for the protection of the city. The message revealed to Zechariah is that Jerusalem does not need walls because Yahweh Himself will protect it when His presence returns to the city (see note on Zech 2:1–13).

I will be the glory in its midst Refers to the glory of Yahweh that inhabited the tabernacle and temple (Exod 40:35; 1 Kgs 8:11).

  In Isaiah, God’s glory among His people often symbolized salvation and restoration (see Isa 4:5; 24:23; 60:18–19).



 2:6–13 The third vision is followed by prophetic oracles emphasizing themes from the earlier visions: the restoration of Israel and judgment on the nations. The oracle in Zech 2:6–9 calls for Jewish exiles still living among the nations to return to Zion before Yahweh judges those nations. The oracle in vv. 10–12 emphasizes Yahweh’s intention to return to Zion and gather Israel and the nations to worship together. The section ends by affirming that Yahweh has set His plan in motion to fulfill the promises of salvation and restoration (v. 13).
           Zechariah 2:6–13 TOTC HagZecMal


2:6 Flee from Isaiah and Jeremiah also commanded the nation to return to Judah following the exile (e.g., Isa 48:20; Jer 31:16–21). See note on Ezra 1:3.

the land of the north May refer to Babylon, but this phrase probably is used figuratively for the foreign lands where Jewish exiles were scattered.

  Jeremiah refers to the north as the direction from which both invasion and returning exiles would come (Jer 3:18; 6:22; 10:22; 16:15; 31:8). The reference to the four winds here suggests the prophet’s appeal is directed at all Jewish exiles in all lands where they have settled (compare Jer 23:8). Isaiah also depicts the gathering of God’s people from all directions (Isa 43:5–6; 49:12).

          North DBI

          North EDB

the four winds of the heavens Referring to the four directions on the compass or indicating that the exiles are spread out in the same way as Yahweh’s messengers who cover the entire world in their patrols (compare Zech 6:5–7; Dan 7:2). See note on Zech 6:5.

          Four DBI

2:8 nations plundering you A reference to the second vision (see 1:19).

the apple of his eye Refers to the opening of the eye—the pupil. The prophet expresses that harming Israel is equal to harming the most vulnerable and sensitive part of Yahweh Himself. Compare Deut 32:10.

  The term babah occurs only here in the Bible. It is a cognate of the Akkadian term babu, meaning “gate.”

2:10 O daughter of Zion A metaphor for the inhabitants of Jerusalem. See note on Isa 1:8.

2:11 on that day Refers to the day that Yahweh returns to Jerusalem.

they will be my people The Israelites were the original people of Yahweh, the nation He redeemed from Egypt (see Exod 3:7; 5:1; 6:7). This verse proclaims that foreigners have the same opportunity to become the people of God. See note on Jer 30:22; note on Ezek 11:20; compare Zech 8:7–8.

  The covenant formula (Lev 26:12) that had reflected Israel’s special relationship with Yahweh will one day be extended to include all nations. Prophetic oracles of restoration often hinted at Yahweh’s plan for future rule of the nations where the nations would also worship Yahweh in Jerusalem (e.g., Isa 56:6–7). See Isa 2:2–4; note on Zech 2:2.

          Zechariah 2:11 Zechariah (NAC)

2:12 will inherit The Hebrew verb used here commonly appears in legal texts of the OT in reference to a property acquisition or an inheritance. See note on Ezek 36:12.

          Nachal WSOTDICT

his portion Refers to an inheritance.

the holy land This is the only occurrence of the Hebrew phrase used here in the OT, but the same concept of sacred space appears elsewhere in the Hebrew text with designations such as “holy ground” (Exod 3:5), “holy mountain” (Isa 11:9), or “holy place” (Ezra 9:8).

          Sacred Space LTW

2:13 Be silent, all people, before Yahweh This verse is an allusion (or indirect refe');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, 0, '3:1–7 Zechariah’s fourth vision describes the nation’s cleansing from sin and the reinstatement of its priestly office and functions (compare Exod 19:6). The vision reestablishes the authority of the Zadokite priesthood and addresses some of the criticisms toward it in the last part of Isaiah (which also seems to address the situation of the early postexilic community; compare Isa 57:1–10; 58:1–5). Zechariah’s vision emphasizes the purification of the high priest, allowing him to properly represent the people before Yahweh.
           Zechariah 3:1–7 NM


3:1 Joshua Refers to Joshua son of Jehozadak (also rendered “Jozadak”), the first high priest of the remnant that returned from exile.

  The Hebrew name for Joshua, yehoshua'', means “Yahweh is salvation” or “Yahweh saves.” A shortened spelling is used in Ezra and Nehemiah (see Ezra 3:2). The name “Jesus” derives from this name.

          Jeshua HIBD



  Joshua son of Jehozadak
 As the first high priest of the returned remnant, Joshua reestablished proper worship of Yahweh by building an altar and resuming sacrificial worship (ca. 537 BC; Ezra 3:1–6). He is identified elsewhere as the son of Jehozadak (or “Jozadak”; Hag 1:1; Ezra 3:2). His first name is sometimes spelled as “Jeshua” (e.g., Ezra 3:2 uses yeshua in Hebrew, not yehoshua as in Zech 3:1). His father was among the priests taken into exile after the fall of Jerusalem in 586 BC (1 Chr 6:15). His grandfather, Seraiah, who had been high priest when Jerusalem fell, was put to death by Nebuchadnezzar (2 Kgs 25:18–21; 1 Chr 6:14). Joshua plays an important role when the prophets Haggai and Zechariah remind the exiles of their need to finish rebuilding the temple (Ezra 5:1–2; Hag 1:1–15).


high priest Joshua was of the priestly family of Jedaiah, one of the 24 priestly families that David organized (1 Chr 24:7).

the angel of Yahweh Representing Yahweh Himself in the vision (see note on Zech 1:11). Since the Angel of Yahweh can either be a representative of Yahweh or the embodiment of Yahweh on earth, the scene here could include either three or four figures: the Angel of Yahweh, Satan, and the priest, or those three plus Yahweh Himself.

Satan Literally “the adversary.” See note on Job 1:6.

        Satan in the Old Testament and the Serpent of Genesis 3

  accuse him The verbal form of the term satan means “to act as an adversary.” Satan’s accusation likely centered on the high priest’s ritual impurity (see Zech 3:3). Leviticus 22:3 commanded that an unclean priest was to be cut off (see note on Lev 7:20).

3:2 a stick A stick used for stirring fires. Illustrates that God graciously rescued Joshua (and the rest of the remnant) just as he was about to be destroyed.

3:3 filthy garments Describes garments stained with excrement, representing Joshua’s sinfulness and ritual impurity.

3:4 the ones standing before him Probably refers to other angels, though human associates of the priest are possible.

will clothe you with rich garments The priests were to wear specially consecrated garments (see Lev 8:7–13, 30; 16:4).

3:5 a clean headband A headdress formed by wrapping long strips of cloth around the head. The turban was part of the priest’s uniform (Lev 8:9).

          Turban EDB

3:7 you will walk in my ways A common metaphor for obedience. Joshua’s reinstatement is conditional. He must keep Yahweh’s laws in order to keep his position of leadership (Exod 18:20; Deut 5:33; 10:12). Compare the change in leadership over a similar failure in 1 Sam 8:1–5.

you will judge Denotes the act of judging. The priests’ duties also extended to deciding legal issues (Deut 17:9). Their decisions involved maintaining the ritual purity of the temple (compare Ezek 44:23–24).

passageways The high priest was the only person authorized to enter the holiest part of the temple (here, “my house” and “my courts”). This access was permitted once a year on the Day of Atonement (Lev 16:29–33).



 3:8–10 This oracle expands on the vision in Zech 3:1–7 by placing the leadership of Joshua (see note on v. 1) within the context of Yahweh’s full plan of restoration, culminating in the coming of the Messiah. The imagery in v. 8 connects the messianic expectations of Isa 4:2–6 with the Suffering Servant figure from Isa 42–53.
           Zechariah 3:8–10 NM


3:8 your companions that are sitting before you These friends are probably distinct from the standing ones of Zech 3:4, 7. They likely are associates of the priest, perhaps even priests themselves.

a sign The Hebrew word used here can refer to a miraculous sign (Exod 4:21; 11:9), a celestial omen (Joel 2:30), or a symbolic event, person, or action (Isa 8:18; 20:3; Ez');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, 0, '4:1–14 Zechariah’s fifth vision is a symbolic picture of the Spirit of Yahweh as He gives power to the anointed ones currently in view—probably Zerubbabel and Joshua. This vision has some similarities to the fourth vision, including a reference to seven eyes, a reference to a stone for the temple, and an emphasis on a historical person.
   In this vision, everything Zechariah sees holds significance. The vision is presented almost as if it were a dream, with an angelic guide explaining the fantastic imagery (compare Dan 8:18; 10:9–10). Zechariah is shown an unusual lampstand—one that is different from a typical menorah or temple lampstand. Zechariah asks three times about it and the two olive trees next to it. In the cryptic fashion typical of apocalyptic visions (compare Dan 7:15–28), the angel’s explanation offers little clarity, emphasizing simply that Yahweh has chosen Zerubbabel for this mission to rebuild the temple and that Yahweh Himself will overcome the worldly obstacles to its reconstruction.
           Zechariah 4:1–14 NM
           Zechariah 4:1–14 TOTC HagZecMal


4:2 a lampstand all of gold Describes a column that supported one or more oil lamps. The lampstands mentioned in the OT were almost always made of gold and designed to be used in religious worship settings (compare 2 Kgs 4:10).

  This lampstand consisted of a large bowl to hold the oil with seven smaller receptacles around the rim of the large bowl; each smaller receptacle had seven notches to hold the wicks for the seven lights. The entire lampstand would have had 49 lights.

          Zechariah 4:2 TOTC HagZecMal

          Zechariah 4:2 ZIBBC OT 5

          Lampstand EDB

a bowl A receptacle for the olive oil.

seven lips on each of Refers to spouts or pipes. Bowls used for lamps were often pinched seven times around the rim to form a notch where the wick could be held.

4:3 two olive trees Ancients used olive oil as fuel for lamps. The surprising thing about this lampstand was that it was fed with oil directly from the trees through the golden pipes (Zech 4:12). The olive, as food and fuel, was an important part of the ancient Near Eastern economy.

          Olive EDB

4:6 Zerubbabel A descendant of King David (1 Chr 3:17–19; Matt 1:12), as well as the governor of Judah (Hag 1:1) who oversaw the rebuilding of the temple (Ezra 3:2, 8; 5:2; see note on Ezra 3:2).

my Spirit Emphasizing that divine power—not human power—will enable them to overcome the opposition to rebuilding the temple. Throughout the OT, God’s Spirit gives divine empowerment to the individuals God selects (e.g., Exod 31:3; Num 11:25–26; 24:2; Judg 3:10; 6:34; 11:29; 1 Sam 10:10; 16:13).

          Spirit EDB

4:7 O great mountain A metaphor for a difficult task. The mountain symbolizes all the challenges that the community will face as they attempt to complete the work that God commanded. The vision serves to reassure them that Yahweh is on their side and will help them overcome the obstacles.

Before Zerubbabel you will become level ground Indicates that Zerubbabel, through the power of God’s Spirit (Zech 4:6), will successfully complete the difficult task Yahweh gave to him.

  In Matthew’s Gospel, Jesus notes the ability of Yahweh to move a mountain, showing God’s power to do the impossible for those who have faith (Matt 17:20).

the top stone Represents the final stone of the temple that Zerubbabel will build (Zech 4:9).

Grace, grace Acknowledges that Yahweh’s Spirit—not human might—was responsible for completing the temple project (v. 6).

4:9 The hands of Zerubbabel The initial effort to rebuild the temple was organized by Zerubbabel (Ezra 3:8–13). He had helped start the work by personally rebuilding the altar alongside the high priest Joshua and the other priests (see Ezra 3:1–3).

his hands will finish it Under the leadership of Zerubbabel, the remnant would continue to work on the temple for the next four years. They completed the project on the third day of Adar in the sixth year of Darius (March 12, 515 BC; see Ezra 6:15).



4:10 plumb line The Hebrew term used here seems to refer to a string with a weight attached to the end. Ancients used tools like plumb lines to determine whether a wall was straight.

  If a wall was leaning, it was more likely to collapse unless it was repaired. The image connotes destruction and reconstruction. If the wall was too damaged to repair, it would be demolished and rebuilt. Compare Amos 7:7 and note.

          Plumb Line EDB

the eyes of Yahweh Symbolizing Yahweh’s awareness of all things. See note on Zech 3:9.

4:11 these two olive trees Zechariah has to ask twice (v. 12) for clarification on the significance of the two trees. See note on v. 3.

4:12 these two twigs of The Hebrew term here, meaning “ears,” is usually used in the sense of ears of grain (Gen 41:5).

golden pipes Allowed the olive oil to run from the trees into the bowl reservoir.

4:14 the two anointed ones The Hebrew text here reads “sons of fresh oil.” The Israelites commonly used olive oil for anointing (Exod 29:7; 1 Sam 16:12–13), which served to set an individual apart for a special purpose.

  Only two types of officials were anointed in the OT: kings and high priests. The “anointed ones” here are probably Zerubbabel and Joshua, the representatives of David and Aaron in this renewed covenant community. Compare note on Zech 6:11.

          Anoint HBD



 5:1–4 Zechariah’s sixth vision illustrates Yahweh’s judgment or curse against all who have sinned by violating His commandments. Just as the fourth vision dealt with the cleansing of the priest’s sin (see 3:1–5), this imagery is likely related to the need for all to be cleansed from their sins brought on by breaking Yahweh’s covenant. The vision addresses the sins of social injustice and religious apostasy. Sin must be dealt with before Yahweh can return and dwell among His people (2:10).
           Zechariah 5:1–4 NM


5:1 a flying scroll This scroll descends from God’s presence in heaven. The writte');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, 0, '5:1–4 Zechariah’s sixth vision illustrates Yahweh’s judgment or curse against all who have sinned by violating His commandments. Just as the fourth vision dealt with the cleansing of the priest’s sin (see 3:1–5), this imagery is likely related to the need for all to be cleansed from their sins brought on by breaking Yahweh’s covenant. The vision addresses the sins of social injustice and religious apostasy. Sin must be dealt with before Yahweh can return and dwell among His people (2:10).
           Zechariah 5:1–4 NM


5:1 a flying scroll This scroll descends from God’s presence in heaven. The written word plays an important role in prophetic and apocalyptic vision imagery (e.g., Ezek 2:8–3:4; Hab 2:2; Isa 8:1; Dan 5:7–25; Rev 5:1–5; compare note on Ezek 2:8).

          Writing HIBD

          Scroll DBI

5:2 twenty cubits long Approximately 30–34 feet. A normal cubit was about 18 inches. A “royal” or “great” cubit was about 21 inches.

ten cubits wide About 15–17 feet. The length of a scroll would usually be many times its width, measured from top to bottom. This heavenly scroll is of immense size. A typical scroll might be 30–35 feet long unrolled, but the writing surface was usually only about 10–12 inches wide.

  Zechariah either sees an unrolled document (written on both sides) that is roughly 15 by 30 feet in size, or a rolled scroll with a writing length of 30 feet (compared to the usual 12 inches) and a rolled diameter of 15 feet. The latter would provide a much greater overall writing surface. A scroll with writing on both sides was likely made from papyrus, not parchment. See note on Ezek 2:10.

          Paper, Papyrus HIBD

5:3 curse The Hebrew term used here alludes to the punishments associated with breaking the covenant (see Deut 29:10–29; Ezek 16:59).

  A formal ancient Near Eastern covenant or treaty would have the consequences for breaking the treaty spelled out in detail in a section toward the end of the document. The curse, therefore, has a judicial or legal function, encouraging the parties to keep their part of the agreement. This scroll appears to contain only the curses or punishments for breaking the law, similar to Ezekiel’s scroll in Ezek 2:10.

          Curse DDD

          Curse TLOT

everyone who steals Compare Exod 20:15.

everyone who swears Refers to misusing the name of Yahweh (see Exod 20:7).

on the other side A scroll with writing on both sides was uncommon (see note on Ezek 2:10). The two sides of the scroll reflect the two main types of sin: sin against a neighbor (or another person) and sin against God.

  The Ten Commandments (Exod 20:1–17) break down along two major categories: laws governing the people’s relationship with Yahweh and laws governing their relationships with each other. One side of the scroll in Zechariah’s vision contained a curse against misusing the name of God. The other side contained a curse against all who steal. Since each is the middle command (the third and eighth) of these two parts of the Ten Commandments, they are probably meant to represent the entire Law.

          Ten Commandments EDB

5:4 it will spend the night in that house It will remain until its intended mission is accomplished. This curse will be in effect until the land is cleansed of sin.

and will destroy it, with its timber and its stone Probably a reference to an individual’s life; God will strike down those who violate His commands.



 5:5–11 The seventh vision presents Zechariah with the strange sight of a woman imprisoned in a barrel or basket. The woman personifies wickedness and is about to be taken to the land of Babylon. The passage reveals that the land of Babylon will become a center of evil when the woman is established there as an idol for those who worship evil.
   The personification of evil or wickedness in female form has parallels with the image of the forbidden woman in Prov 7:5–27 and the great prostitute of Rev 17. The imagery focuses on evil as a power in and of itself—an external, objective force, not merely the sinful actions of people. God alone has supreme power over evil, directing His angels to remove it to distant Shinar (also known as Babylon or Babylonia), a location symbolic of rebellious opposition to Yahweh (Gen 11:2; compare Rev 14:8).
           Zechariah 5:5–11 NM


5:6 a basket The Hebrew word ephah used here is a technical term for a unit used to measure volume (both dry and liquid). It often denotes “basket” throughout Zech 5:6–11. Estimates of the capacity vary from around 0.8 cubic feet to 1.25 cubic feet (22 to 36 liters).

  Identifying the capacity of the basket in this passage is puzzling because it is too small for a container holding a woman (v. 7). It is possible that this passage does not indicate the unit of measure but relates to Mesopotamian religion. One proposal based on possible Semitic cognates is that ephah refers to the small room or shrine that sat on top of a ziggurat. The destination of the basket as described in v. 11 favors this possibility. On the other hand, a prophetic vision could include larger-than-life elements (like the flying scroll of v. 2 that measured at least 30 feet by 15 feet). Thus, the size of what Zechariah saw relative to the size of a typical ephah may be irrelevant.

their iniquity The Hebrew word here literally means “their eye.”

  Many English translations follow the Greek and Syriac translations of the OT, which appear to have read “their iniquity.” The difference in spelling is only one letter in ancient Hebrew, and the two letters in question (vav and yod) looked very similar in handwritten texts. The meaning “iniquity” makes sense in light of the description of the basket’s contents as “wickedness” in v. 8.

5:7 a woman The representation of evil as a woman being carried to Babylon likely lies behind the symbolism of Rev 14:8. See note on Zech 5:5–11.

5:8 Wickedness The term used here refers to evil in general (Deut 9:4–5; Prov 11:5; Ezek 18:27; Mal 3:18).

the lead cover Since lead is the heaviest of all common metals, its great weight causes the basket to become a cage or prison for the woman (who is personified wickedness).

5:9 the wind The Hebrew term here may be used with the connotation of “spirit,” indicating that God’s Spirit was empowering these women (compare Zech 4:6).

their wings Indicates the women are likely heavenly beings.

a stork A large, wading bird that has been noted from ancient times as a bird that takes affectionate care of its offspring. The stork was a high-flying bird.

  The idea that the stork is affectionate is reflected in the stork’s name; the word chasidah, meaning “stork,” comes from the word chasid, meaning “loving,” or “faithful.” Storks were unclean for consumption according to Lev 11:13, 19.

          Stork HBD

5:11 Shinar This land was known variously as “Babylon,” “Babylonia,” or “Shinar” (Gen 10:10; see note on Ezra 1:11).

  The Bible, at times, uses Babylon as a symbol for the center of humankind’s wickedness (e.g., Gen 11:1–9; Isa 13–14; Jer 50–51). Babylon eventually will be depicted as the center of Satan’s earthly kingdom (Rev 17–18).

          Shinar EDB



 6:1–8 The eighth and final vision illustrates the universal reign of Yahweh. This vision parallels the opening vision (Zech 1:7–17): Both feature the number four, horses of various colors, a valley—all with an emphasis on the entire earth. The two visions function as an inclusio, bracketing the sequence of visions in chs. 1–6.
           Zechariah 6:1–8 NM
           Zechariah 6:1–15 TOTC HagZecMal
           Zechariah 6:1–8 Zechariah (NAC)


6:1 four chariots Compare with the four horses of 1:8. Each chariot was probably pulled by two horses. See note on Nah 2:3.

          Chariots HIBD

between The chariots progress through a valley, probably symbolic of the entrance of Yahweh’s abode. The chariots representing His divine power are sent out from His holy mountain.

two mountains The two bronze mountains function symbolically like the two bronze');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, 0, '6:1–8 The eighth and final vision illustrates the universal reign of Yahweh. This vision parallels the opening vision (Zech 1:7–17): Both feature the number four, horses of various colors, a valley—all with an emphasis on the entire earth. The two visions function as an inclusio, bracketing the sequence of visions in chs. 1–6.
           Zechariah 6:1–8 NM
           Zechariah 6:1–15 TOTC HagZecMal
           Zechariah 6:1–8 Zechariah (NAC)


6:1 four chariots Compare with the four horses of 1:8. Each chariot was probably pulled by two horses. See note on Nah 2:3.

          Chariots HIBD

between The chariots progress through a valley, probably symbolic of the entrance of Yahweh’s abode. The chariots representing His divine power are sent out from His holy mountain.

two mountains The two bronze mountains function symbolically like the two bronze pillars framing the entrance to the temple (compare 1 Kgs 7:15–22).

  The symbolism of the two mountains also might parallel ancient Near Eastern imagery of a sun god rising between the mountains. The bronze color represented the sunlight reflecting on the mountains. There may be a connection to Zech 14:4, which describes how two mountains are created when Yahweh stands on the Mount of Olives and it splits in two.

          Zechariah 6:1 TOTC HagZecMal

          Zechariah 6:1 ZIBBC OT 5

bronze This metal alloy made from the combination of copper and tin was commonly used in fortifications and could reinforce gateways (Jer 1:18).

6:3 strong dappled horses The term used here could also refer to the degree to which the horses of the fourth chariot were spotted—they were “strongly dappled.” The variety of colored horses compares with the vision of Zech 1:8 and likely influenced the imagery in Rev 6:2–5.

6:5 the four winds of the heavens The Hebrew term ruach is likely used here to denote “spirit” rather than “wind” (see note on Ezek 37:5; compare Ezek 37:9). Some translations phrase this passage to imply the chariots are being sent out to the four winds—that is, in all four directions. The underlying Hebrew phrase, however, clearly equates the chariots with the winds.

  The number four represents the whole of the created order and often appears in depictions of divine judgment. The four chariots represent the four winds (or “spirits”) of heaven, Yahweh’s messengers of judgment (compare Jer 49:36; Dan 7:2; Psa 104:4). Zechariah asks, “What are these?” in Zech 6:4, referring to the chariots he saw in vv. 1–3. The angel answers, “These are the four winds of heaven going forth” (v. 5). Another difficulty with equating the winds with four directions comes in v. 6, where the chariots are sent in only two directions—north and south.

          Four DBI

          Wind DBI

presenting themselves before the Lord of Probably in the sense of standing before the divine council (or heavenly court) for commissioning. The same verb form is used in Job 1:6 describing the “sons of God” gathering before Yahweh. See note on Job 1:6. Compare Exod 19:17; Num 11:16; Deut 31:14; Judg 20:2.

6:6 the north country Symbolic of the location of Yahweh’s enemies throughout much of the OT (compare Zech 2:6 and note). The greater importance of the north as symbolic of evil is indicated by the two chariots heading that direction; in comparison, only one heads south.

  Jeremiah describes divine judgment coming from the north (Jer 1:14 and note). Ezekiel also emphasizes the northern county as the home of Israel’s eschatological enemy who will invade at the end of days (Ezek 38:14–16). The imagery probably developed out of the reality that Israel was usually subject to catastrophic invasion from the north by different Mesopotamian powers (e.g., Babylon, Assyria). The Canaanite god Baal was also depicted living on a mountain in the north (see note on Isa 2:2). North also indicated the left side, based on the eastward orientation common to the ancient Near East (see Gen 14:15; Ezek 16:46). In Semitic cultures, the left side is associated with calamity. These factors made the north country symbolic of all that was evil and sinister in the world. The end of the vision in Zech 6:8 indicates that these divine messengers subdued the evil power of the north, making the world ready for Yahweh’s presence to return.

          North DBI

the south country Possibly refers to Egypt. Israel’s location, with the Mediterranean to the west and the desert to the east, meant that large-scale invasion only came from the south (Egypt) or the north. The most destructive invasions came from the north. The opposing powers of north and south play an important role in the vision of Dan 11:2–45.

  The south can represent both positive and hostile elements. As the opposite of the hostile “north,” it can be construed in a favorable way. Yahweh is also depicted as dwelling in the south or coming to Israel out of the south, connected to the southern mountains such as Mount Sinai, Paran, Teman, or Seir (Deut 33:2; Judg 5:4–5; Hab 3:3). The south was also associated with the inhospitable desert of the Negev and, thus, could be used as a symbol of judgment (Zech 9:14; Isa 21:1; 30:6; Job 37:17). The country of Egypt also represented a traditional enemy of Israel, the site of their former slavery and oppression (Deut 5:6, 15).

          South DBI

          Egypt DBI

6:7 patrol Compare Zech 1:10–11, where God’s messengers had just returned from patrolling the earth.



 6:9–15 This oracle serves as a climax to the eight night visions and reiterates Yahweh’s choice of Joshua as high priest (see note on 3:1–7). Zechariah is commanded to make a crown for Joshua out of silver and gold. This symbolic act establishes the priests as the divinely appointed leaders of the community. The emphasis on the temple and religious leadership reflects the realistic situation of the exiles, who had more freedom over religious matters than political affairs. The passage also serves to connect the visions of chs. 1–6 with the oracles of chs. 7–8.
           Zechariah 6:9–15 NM


        Symbolic Actions of the Prophets Table

6:9 the word of Yahweh Identifying this passage as a prophetic oracle. See note on 1:1.

6:10 from the exiles Many Jews remained in Babylon instead of returning to the land of Israel (see note on Ezra 1:3), so there were new returnees joining the community for some time (see Ezra 4:12 and note). These three men are possibly the leaders or at least the most prominent, wealthy members of a group that recently arrived from Babylon. They provide the precious metal that will be used to fashion a crown for Joshua, suggesting they were wealthy.

6:11 a crown A headpiece signifying royalty or a position of honor. The priest’s headdress is described in Exod 29:6; 39:30.

  The word used here is the usual term for a royal crown (2 Sam 12:30; Psa 21:3; Jer 13:18). It is otherwise never used for the headdress of a priest. The terminology here serves to subtly link the high priest with royal authority. The word is grammatically plural, raising questions over how many crowns were made. See note on Zech 6:12.

          Zechariah 6:11 Zechariah (NAC)

          Crown HBD

          Crown DBI

set it on the head of Joshua The identification of the messianic Branch with a priest (v. 12) foreshadows the uniting of kingship and priesthood in the person of the Messiah (compare Psa 110:2, 4).

  This passage probably inspired expectations of a priestly Messiah in Second Temple Judaism. The group responsible for the sectarian Dead Sea Scrolls expected a priestly messiah in addition to a prophet like Moses and a royal Davidic messiah (see the documents Rule of the Community 9.11; the Rule of the Congregation 2.12–22; and the Melchizedek Scroll). The author of Hebrews makes a similar connection when proclaiming Jesus’ high priesthood (Heb 2:17–18; compare note on John 1:21). For more on Joshua, see note on Zech 3:1.

          Two Messiahs AI:IJAL

          Beliefs of the Qumran Sect BEB

6:12 Branch See note on 3:8.

he will build the temple of Yahweh Refers to the temple in Jerusalem. Zechariah’s vision in 4:1–14 had identified Zerubbabel as the one who would successfully complete the temple rebuilding project (see 4:9), but he is not directly in view in this prophecy. Instead, it is Joshua the high priest who is emphasized.

  The intertwining themes of priestly and royal authority could be more appropriately applied to the two separate figures of Joshua and Zerubbabel (see note on 4:14). Zerubbabel may be in view in the vision, as the Branch, since the two leaders are almost always mentioned together in Ezra and Nehemiah (see note on 3:1). According to this interpretation, the crown from v. 11 may refer to two or more crowns. The use of two precious metals—silver and gold—could also point to the existence of two crowns. However, Zerubbabel never rules a politically independent nation, which the text seems to imply as necessary. It could be that a completely different figure is in view.

  The ultimate fulfillment of this imagery comes when Jesus, from the line of David, unites the roles of priest and king to truly establish Yahweh’s kingdom on earth (Heb 3:3; Eph 2:20–22).

          Zechariah 6:12 Zechariah (NAC)

6:13 will build the temple of Yahweh The repetition of this phrase from the end of');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, 0, '7:1–14 This passage addresses the practical implications of Judah’s renewed faith in Yahweh. The occasion of the oracles in Zech 7:4–14 is the inquiry of a delegation from Bethel regarding the observance of fast days commemorating the siege and destruction of Jerusalem and the temple (vv. 1–3). Zechariah’s first message criticizes these fasts as inappropriate to the new beginning symbolized by the reestablished Jewish community (vv. 4–7). The second message emphasizes the importance of ethical living over empty religious observances (vv. 8–14).
           Zechariah 7:1–14 NM


7:1 in the fourth year of King Darius Refers to 518 BC. This final chronological heading in Zechariah indicates that about two years had passed since he delivered his call to repentance (see 1:1 and note) and experienced the eight visions (see 1:7–6:8 and note).





Kislev The ninth month in the Hebrew calendar; aligns roughly to late November and early December. This date formula places these messages in early December, 518 BC.

        Israelite Calendar Table

7:2 the people of Bethel A city about 10 miles north of Jerusalem, near the southern border of the former northern kingdom of Israel.

          Bethel EDB

          Bethel ISBE



  Bethel
 Bethel, which means “house of God,” was an ancient sacred site (see Gen 28:10–22) that was associated with improper worship during the time of the northern kingdom (1 Kgs 12:28–33; Amos 3:14; 4:4; 7:13). Jeroboam, the first king of the northern kingdom, had established Israelite religious centers at Bethel in the southern part of his kingdom and at Dan in the north (1 Kgs 12:28–29). The purpose of the shrine was to discourage people from making a pilgrimage to Jerusalem for worship. Biblical writers routinely criticized the worship at Bethel as improper idolatry, lamenting that the kings of Israel continued this practice begun by Jeroboam (see 1 Kgs 16:25–26; Amos 4:4; 7:7–13).


Sharezer An Assyrian name meaning “protect the king” (2 Kgs 19:37; Isa 37:38).

Regem-Melech Means “friend of the king.”

to entreat the favor of Yahweh This group from Bethel is sent to offer sacrifices at the temple in Jerusalem. The remnant had set up the altar immediately after returning (Ezra 3:1–3) following the decree of Cyrus (Ezra 1:2–4). At this time, they were rebuilding the temple (Ezra 3:10–13).

7:3 Should I mourn The delegation from Bethel was seeking the endorsement of the religious leaders in Jerusalem for their religious observance.

and keep myself separate The Jews had imposed a religious observance commemorating Nebuchadnezzar’s destruction of Jerusalem and the temple in 586 BC (2 Kgs 25:8–9); this involved fasting (see Zech 7:5).

the fifth month The month of Ab, late July and early August.

7:5 seventh Tishri—late September and early October. This observance may have commemorated the murder of Gedaliah, the governor Nebuchadnezzar placed over Judah (2 Kgs 25:22–25). This event precipitated the remnant’s flight to Egypt (2 Kgs 25:26).

seventy years Referring to the time of exile. See note on Jer 25:11.

7:6 drinking for yourselves The religious observance of the inhabitants of Bethel was a matter of posturing rather than piety. They were observing the fast simply to draw attention to themselves instead of offering true repentance to Yahweh.

  Their commemorative observance had become a legalistic ritual instead of an act of heartfelt devotion toward God. They were also mourning the loss of their possessions and their great city instead of their lost relationship with God.

7:7 former prophets Refers to prophets such as Isaiah, Jeremiah, and Micah.

the Negev The desert region south of Jerusalem was called the Negev, meaning “south.”

the Shephelah Jerusalem was located at the top of a mountain; the region to the west of the city was often called the lowland as it was much lower in elevation. This region included the Shephelah and the Philistine plain.

7:9 with trustworthy justice The prophets continually emphasize that the ethical aspects of God’s law are just as important as the ritual aspects (see Isa 1:17 and note; compare Mic 6:8; Matt 23:23).

steadfast love The Hebrew term used here, chesed, is often used in reference to');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, 0, '8:1–23 This chapter contains Zechariah’s final two explanatory messages (see note on Zech 7:1–14). Zechariah’s third message is one of restoration (vv. 1–17). The fourth message is one of rejoicing (vv. 18–23).


8:1 Yahweh of hosts See note on Nah 2:13.

8:2 Zion Another name for Jerusalem. See note on Isa 1:8.

I am jealous for it with great wrath Yahweh is fiercely protective of His people. All nations who come against Israel will experience the wrath of God (see Gen 12:3).

8:3 I will dwell in the midst of Jerusalem A reference to the messianic reign (Psa 2:6), when Yahweh Himself will reign over the entire earth from Jerusalem (Joel 3:16–17; Zech 14:9; see note on Zeph 1:4).

the holy mountain Jerusalem is situated at the top of a mountain. Compare Isaiah 2:2–3; Psa 48:1 and note.

8:4 Old men and old women Symbolizes the peaceful state of the city.

8:5 boys and girls Creates a merism with the old men and women of Zech 8:4.

8:6 it seems impossible to the remnant of Zechariah depicts his audience as skeptical of this kind of transformation. At this time, Jerusalem was largely unpopulated and full of ruins (see Neh 7:4).

8:7 my people Refers to the Israelites, whom Yahweh redeemed from Egypt (Exod 3:7, 10; 5:1; 6:7; 7:4, 16; 8:1).

the land of the east, and from the land of the west Represents of all nations on the earth.

8:8 they will dwell in the midst of Jerusalem The gathering of the Jews to the land of Israel is a common theme in the prophets (e.g., Ezek 20:33–44; Amos 9:14–15; Zeph 3:20).

They will be my people Compare Hosea 2:23.

in faithfulness and in righteousness This situation will be the opposite of Isa 48:1, which uses the same words.

8:9 your hands This phrase forms an inclusio (a bracketing by repetition) with the end of Zech 8:13.

in these days Refers to Zechariah’s present time.

the mouth of the prophets Includes Haggai and Zechariah, who were instrumental in encouraging the Jewish people to restart the rebuilding of the temple in 520 BC (see Ezra 5:1–2). The Jewish people halted the rebuilding project shortly after rebuilding the altar in 536 BC, because they feared their enemies (Ezra 4:1–5).



the house of Yahweh of The temple in Jerusalem.

8:10 those days Refers to the days when the temple project was restarted. The rebuilding of the temple resumed in 520 BC after a period of inactivity (compare note on Zech 8:9).

wage for people or for animals Refers to the economic distress that took place while the temple lay in ruins (see Hag 1:5–11).

8:11 the remnant of this people Referring to the returnees. See note on Isa 1:9; compare note on Jer 24:8.

the former days Refers to the days prior to the commencement of the rebuilding project when the temple lay in ruins.

8:12 a sowing of peace Yahweh will allow the produce of the ground to grow without destroying it (see Hag 1:10–11).

the vine will give its fruit Refers to a grapevine and grapes. Compare Haggai 1:11.

          Vine HBD

the soil will give its produce A reference to grain, probably wheat and barley. Compare Haggai 1:10.

the heavens will give their dew Compare Haggai 1:10.

I will cause the remnant of this people to inherit all these things Indicates that Yahweh will give the Israelites bountiful harvests.

8:13 O house of Judah and house of Israel A collective reference to the Israelites of the northern and southern kingdoms.

let your hands be strong See note on Zech 8');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, 0, '9:1–17 This oracle proclaims Yahweh’s judgment against Israel’s neighbors (Zech 9:1–8) and emphasizes His concern to protect His people (vv. 11–17). The list of cities in vv. 1–7 proceeds generally from north to south, and v. 8 depicts Yahweh’s return to His temple in Jerusalem. The passage ends with Yahweh establishing complete peace and security (vv. 11–17). Two verses in the middle of the chapter announce the return of a king to Zion who takes his place to rule over a now-pacified world (vv. 9–10). This king’s arrival brings the ultimate peace over all the earth that will follow Yahweh’s total restoration of His rule (Isa 2:4; Hos 2:18). The entire oracle reflects the imagery of the Divine Warrior battling on behalf of His people.


9:1 An oracle The Hebrew term used here (massa) can refer both to a burden (e.g., Exod 23:5; Deut 1:12) and oracle or prophecy (e.g., Ezek 12:10; Isa 13:1; Hab 1:1). It is frequently used to identify prophetic oracles (Nah 1:1; Isa 15:1; 17:1; 19:1). The prophets may use this word ironically, referring to the oracle as a burden that they must carry or implying that the message is a burden for the audience to bear (see Jer 23:33 and note).

the land of Hadrach A city in northern Syria that is located about 30 miles southwest of Aleppo. Identified with modern Tell Afis. This is the only mention of this place in the OT.

          Hadrach EDB

Damascus A city in southern Syria; the chief city of the Arameans.

9:2 Hamath A city in northern Syria that is located on the Orontes River about 130 miles north of Damascus.

  This city, which is mentioned many times in extrabiblical documents, was a center of trade due to its strategic location on the major trade routes connecting Africa with Asia Minor. Hamath is to be identified with the modern Hama.

          Hamath EDB

Tyre The most important Phoenician city. See note on Neh 13:16; note on Ezek 26:2.

Sidon A city in central Phoenicia (modern Lebanon). Located about 25 miles south of modern-day Beirut. Jezebel was from Sidon.

          Sidon EDB

they are very wise Likely mocking Tyre and Sidon for their confidence in their own abilities. Ezekiel similarly mocks the ruler of Tyre for his arrogant reliance on his own wisdom (see Ezek 28:3–5).

9:3 a fortification A heavily fortified defensive stronghold.

gold like the mud of the streets Tyre became incredibly wealthy as a center of trade and commerce.

9:4 will drive it out Tyre’s downfall is depicted with similar imagery in Ezek 26:4–5.



 9:5–6 The four cities mentioned here were part of the “Philistine pentapolis” (Gaza, Gath, Ekron, Ashkelon, and Ashdod). See note on Zeph 2:4.
           Biblical Places
           Philistines EDB


        Philistines

9:6 the pride of the Philistines Refers to the wealth and power of the nation. Everything the Philistines took pride in would be destroyed when Yahweh came in judgment (compare Isa 13:19; 16:6; Jer 13:9; Hos 5:5; 7:10).

9:7 its blood from its mouth Perhaps intimating that the Philistines consumed the blood, something Yahweh had forbidden (Gen 9:4; Lev 3:17). The blood is likely that of animal sacrifices offered to idols.

abominations The Hebrew word here is commonly used to refer to idols, associating them with something disgusting or abhorrent (see 1 Kgs 11:7; 2 Kgs 23:24). Here, it probably refers to meat sacrificed to idols.

          Idolatry LTW

the Jebusite A tribe of unknown origin who were the inhabitants of Jerusalem (Jebus) before David captured the city in 1004 BC.

          Jebus HBD

9:8 I will encamp at my temple Refers to the temple in Jerusalem. Yahweh will return to dwell in Jerusalem and protect His people from oppression by foreign nations.



 9:9–10 Zechariah draws on the language and imagery of a number of other OT passages in these two verses. His description of the messianic king echoes passages from Isaiah and Micah (e.g., Isa 9:6–7; 11:1–5; Mic 5:2–4). The extent of the king’s power and influence also contains echoes of Psa 72 (especially Psa 72:1–11). The nations’ acceptance of Judah’s rule and the references to a donkey and a donkey’s colt are likely allusions to Gen 49:10–11.
           Zechariah 9:9–10 WBCV32:MM
           Zechariah 9:9 Zechariah (NAC)


9:9 O daughter of Zion Refers to the inhabitants of Jerusalem (see note on Isa 1:8).

Your king Refers to the Messiah (compare Jer 23:5).

righteous and victorious Yahweh has equipped the coming messianic king with righteousness (Isa 9:7; 11:4–5; compare Psa 72:1). He will fulfill Yahweh’s ideal for kingship (see note on Jer 22:3; compare Deut 10:18).

a donkey An animal similar to a horse that is used for riding or as a beast of burden. A donkey was a common mount for transportation in biblical times (see Num 22:21; Judg 5:10; 2 Sam 16:2). In this context, the reference to a donkey is likely meant evoke th');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, 0, '10:1 the season of the spring rain The rains that commence in March or April are known as the spring rain or latter rain. The former rains are those of autumn that follow the harvest time. See Joel 2:23 and note.

10:2 household gods The Hebrew term used here (teraphim) is also used for the household idols that Rachel steals from her father, Laban (see Gen 31:19 and note). These idols might have been used for ancestor worship.

          Background Studies: Household Gods LRC:G1250

those who practice divination Those who use a variety of techniques to determine messages from the gods. Such practices are prohibited in biblical law (Deut 18:10–14). The prophets frequently condemn these methods of attempting to communicate with supernatural beings (see Ezek 13:23; 21:21; Jer 27:9; Mic 3:6–7).

see a lie The prophet appears to be condemning those who gave false hope to God’s people with their optimistic messages (compare Jer 14:13–18; 23:16–17; 27:8–11; 28:15; Ezek 13:1–23).

the people wander like sheep The people were described as God’s flock in Zech 9:16. The metaphor of the people as sheep is fairly common in the OT (see Psa 74:1; 95:7; 100:3; Ezek 34:31).

there is no shepherd Without godly leadership, the people go astray and suffer (compare Ezek 34:5 and note). The leaders should be protecting the people as a shepherd would protect his flock, but they have failed (see 1 Kgs 22:17; Jer 2:8; note on Ezek 34:1–10). In Ezekiel, Yahweh Himself takes over for the failed shepherds (Ezek 34:10–11; compare Psa 78:72), but He also assigns that role to His Messiah (Ezek 34:23; 37:24; Mic 5:4).

10:3 against the shepherds Compare Ezekiel 34:10.

the leaders The Hebrew term here, literally meaning “the he-goats” or “rams,” is being used metaphorically for those who would lead the flock (compare Isa 14:9; Jer 50:8).

10:4 the cornerstone The first stone placed when erecting a building. Each term used in this verse—cornerstone, tent peg or nail, and battle bow—is used metaphorically for the leaders of Israel, who are depicted as great warriors (see Zech 10:5).

10:6 the house of Joseph I will save The northern kingdom of Israel is sometimes referred to as Joseph or Ephraim, using the most powerful northern tribe as a representative of the entire kingdom (compare Psa 78:67).

I have compassion on them The prophets frequently indicate that Yahweh’s compassion for His people motivates Him to restore and redeem them (see Isa 14:1; 54:8; Jer 12:15; 13:14; 31:20; 33:26; Ezek 39:25).

10:7 the people of Ephraim Referring to the northern kingdom of Israel. See note on Hos 4:17.

10:8 I will whistle to them Reflects a shepherd who whistles for his sheep.

10:9 Though I sow them among the nations A common image for Israel’s punishment for breaking the covenant: They were taken captive and resettled in different lands. They were scattered, but Yahweh will bring them back (compare Isa 11:11–12; 27:13; Hos 11:11).

10:11 sea of This imagery of Yahweh’s subjugation of the waters so His people can return home is likely an allusion (or indirect reference) to Isa 11:15.

The pride of Assyria The condemnation of the “pride” of a nation typically refers to whatever that people prized most as a source of identity and distinctiveness. For Assyrians, it likely refers to their military might. See note on Zech 9:6.



 11:1–17');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, 0, '11:1–17 Chapter 11 paints a gloomy picture of death and destruction. The narrative of the shepherd of the sheep for slaughter in vv. 4–16 is surrounded by two poetic oracles in vv. 1–3 and 11:17. The first describes the destruction of the natural beauty of the regions north of Israel—Lebanon, Bashan, and the fertile Jordan Valley. The closing oracle pronounces woe on the shepherd who has failed in his duty to protect his flock. These three segments are unified by the continued use of shepherd imagery, introduced in 10:2–3. The basic message is one of judgment on the people and on their leaders for rejecting Yahweh. In the central narrative, Yahweh orders the prophet to become shepherd of the “sheep for slaughter.” His service appears to symbolize Yahweh’s relationship with Israel through the prophets: They attempt to lead the people back to Yahweh, but the people persist in their obstinate rebellion until both prophet and Yahweh are frustrated.


11:1 O Lebanon The mountainous region north of the Sea of Galilee, known for its cedar trees (see note on Isa 14:8). Cedar was valuable as a building material in the Mediterranean world. It also was the largest and most majestic of the region’s trees.

11:2 O juniper A type of evergreen fir tree known for its thick foliage. Only the cedar trees are destroyed; the cypresses (or junipers, in some translations) and oaks are called to mourn the loss of the glorious cedar forests.

oaks of Bashan Oaks, the other prized tree of the region, often were used as symbols of strength and power. The cedars of Lebanon and oaks of Bashan are similarly paired in Isa 2:13 and Ezek 27:5–6. Amos 2:9 also uses cedar and oak to symbolize height and power, respectively. Bashan is a region of the northern Transjordan known for its fertility (Mic 7:14).

11:3 the thickets of the Jordan The largest and most important river in Canaan. Its headwaters originate in the region of Mount Hermon, and the river flows south to the Dead Sea. The vegetation along the banks of the Jordan River is extremely dense, creating a tropical jungle that contrasted sharply with the surrounding barren terrain and provided a habitat for wildlife, including lions. Jeremiah uses similar language to describe the area (see Jer 12:5; 49:19; 50:44).

11:4 Shepherd The idea of a prophet (Zechariah) as the symbolic representation of his message is common in prophetic literature (e.g., Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel, Hosea, Jonah).

        Symbolic Actions of the Prophets Table

doomed to slaughter Refers to the herd of animals kept outside the temple for sacrificial offering. This image symbolizes that the Israelites are doomed to be conquered by foreign nations.

11:5 Their own shepherds The leaders have taken advantage of the people instead of caring for them (compare Isa 1:23; Amos 5:11–13).

11:6 am going to cause humankind to fall, each into the hand of his neighbor A reference to the conquests of the northern kingdom by Assyria and the southern kingdom by Babylon.

11:7 I shepherded Zechariah is now speaking.

two staffs A long stick used by shepherds to defend sheep against wild animals.

one I called Kindness The staffs are indicative of the shepherd’s guiding principles. The name used here can denote beauty, favor, or delight, symbolizing God’s blessing on His people (Psa 90:17).

the other I called Unity The name can denote harmony, unity, and bands or bonds. Harmony or agreement is the outcome of leadership guided by divine favor.

11:8 three shepherds The identity of these shepherds is unclear. Many possibilities have been suggested, such as the last three kings of Judah: Jehoiakim, Jehoiachin, and Zedekiah.

  It might be preferable to take the three shepherds as symbolic of the ineffective leadership of Israel and Judah as a whole. Three is a common symbolic number that can represent completeness. Their removal after a short period of time could represent divine judgment for their failure.

11:9 let them devour the flesh of each other A reference to the cannibalism that accompanied the siege of Jerusalem (see Deut 28:54–55; Lam 4:10).

11:10 to break my covenant that I had made Israel had broken the terms of Yahweh’s covenant (compare Jer 11:6–11).

11:12 give me my wages Having lost his sheep, the prophet asks the nation to provide his wages.

thirty silver shekels The afflicted nation determined that the service of the shepherd was worth 30 pieces of silver, the legal compensation for the loss of a slave (see Exod 21:32). The reference here is to the shekel, the basic unit of silver that weighed just over 11 grams.

  Thirty shekels of silver was a significant sum. Nehemiah 5:15 refers to 40 shekels as a burdensome tax collected by previous governors. Matthew’s Gospel likely alludes to this passage in recounting the 30 pieces of silver given to Judas Iscariot for betraying Jesus (Matt 26:15). Later, Matthew reports the fulfillment of this prophecy when the priests use the 30 pieces of silver discarded by Judas to buy a potter’s field (Matt 27:3–10).



11:13 Throw it to the potter Refers to the potter’s field, a place of debris where a potter');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, 0, '12:1–14:21 The second revelatory oracle (Zech 12:1–14:21) focuses on the events surrounding Yahweh’s second coming and His enthronement as the King of Israel. The consistent use of the phrase “in that day” demonstrates the future-oriented nature of the oracle.


12:1 who stretches out the heavens, founds the earth Demonstrates Yahweh’s omnipotence.

forms The term used here is commonly used to describe the work of potters as they mold clay.

12:2 a cup of reeling Describes a cup of wine that causes the person drinking it to stagger. The surrounding nations will become like a stumbling, drunken man. Isaiah 51:17, 22 and Jeremiah 25:15–16 also use this metaphor (see note on Jer 25:15).

12:3 that day Refers to the day that Jerusalem becomes a cup of staggering—the day of God’s judgment.

a stone weight Describes a stone that injures those who try to lift it. Those nations who attempt to attack Jerusalem will find the task too difficult and will end up injured.

12:4 my eyes God declares that He will watch over the house of Judah.

12:6 a pan of fire The term used here designates a cooking vessel (1 Sam 2:14) or a basin for water, such as the laver (e.g., Exod 30:18). In this case, the pot is filled with coals that burn the surrounding wood. This imagery alludes back to Zech 11:1–3, where the trees representing kings are burned with fire.

to the right and to the left Since the Israelites orient themselves to the east, the right would be south and the left would be north—the same directions that the chariots of 6:6 went as they traveled out to patrol the earth.

12:7 the tents of Judah A metaphor referring to the towns and villages that surround Jerusalem.

12:8 one who stumbles among them Under Yahweh’s direct protection, even the weak become strong. Compare Isaiah 60:22.

12:10 grace and supplication Grace is special favor freely given by God to the nation, whereas pleas for mercy represent Yahweh’s response to the nation’s petitions. The two together portray the vast extent of divine providence.

whom they pierced The change in pronoun is done for stylistic reasons; the subject is still God. This phrase refers to the heartbreaking effect the nation’s idolatry has on God (compare Zech 13:3).

  John’s Gospel uses this phrase at the end of a sequence of OT allusions and quotations designed to show how the details of Jesus’ crucifixion aligned with biblical prophecy. In John 19:32–37, the soldiers pierce Jesus’ side with a spear, and John identifies that action as the fulfillment of Zech 12:10 (see John 19:37).

          John 19:37 CNTUOT

one wails over an only child The great mourning will resemble the laments that accompanying the loss of an only child.

12:11 Hadad Rimmon The name of the Aramean storm god. However, the term here seems to identify a location near Megiddo. While this location is unknown, the mourning referenced here was for the death of Josiah in 609 BC (2 Chr 35:22–25). Josiah was the last godly king of Judah.

          Hadad-Rimmon EDB

        Pagan Deities in the Old Testament Table



the plain of Megiddo The Plain of Esdraelon was dominated by the powerful fortress city of Megiddo.

          Biblical Places

          Megiddo HBD

12:12 the house of David Refers to the royal family.

the house of Nathan The third son of David born in Jerusalem. Shortly after the exile, the royal line began to be traced through Nathan. (Zerubbabel was of the line of Nathan, not Solomon.)

12:13 the Shimeites The family of Shimei, the grandson of Levi (Num 3:18, 21). Here they represent the priestly line in Israel.

12:14 all those clans Everyone will mourn, from the least to the greatest.

13:1 that day The Day of Yahweh was a day of judgment (Zech 12:2–3) as well as a day of restoration.

the house of David and for the inhabitants of Jerusalem Representative of the whole redeemed people.

cleanse them from sin and from impurity The people must be restored to a state of purity before God. Washing with water was one of the methods for restoring cleanness outlined in biblical law (e.g., Lev 13–14).

13:2 I will cut off the names of the Refers to removing the idols completely so that no');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, 0, '13:1 that day The Day of Yahweh was a day of judgment (Zech 12:2–3) as well as a day of restoration.

the house of David and for the inhabitants of Jerusalem Representative of the whole redeemed people.

cleanse them from sin and from impurity The people must be restored to a state of purity before God. Washing with water was one of the methods for restoring cleanness outlined in biblical law (e.g., Lev 13–14).

13:2 I will cut off the names of the Refers to removing the idols completely so that not even the memory of them remains.

the prophets Refers to the false prophets.

the spirit of uncleanness A spirit that dwells in the false prophets (1 Kgs 22:19–23).

13:3 You shall not live Deuteronomy 13:1–11 commands the death penalty for those who engage in idolatry and false prophecy. In this restored future, loyalty to Yahweh trumps loyalty to family.

a lie in the name of Yahweh Falsely claiming to speak for Yahweh also merited the death penalty (see Deut 18:20).

13:4 they will not put on The false prophet’s shame is demonstrated by his refusal to wear a prophet’s uniform and his denial of being a prophet (Zech 13:5).

13:5 I am not a prophet False prophets will go into hiding and masquerade as farmers. The false prophets’ claim echoes Amos 7:14, where Amos asserts that he is not a professional prophet—just a working man called by God. By contrast, the false prophet is speaking the truth that he is no prophet in actuality; God has not called him, but he is hiding his past—that he once claimed to speak for God.

13:6 these wounds between your arms False prophets often cut themselves as part of their idolatrous rituals. The custom originated as a way to show one’s devotion to the gods.

  The prophets of Baal cut themselves in an attempt to encourage Baal to send fire from heaven (see 1 Kgs 18:28). The law forbade the Israelites from cutting themselves, though the specific reference is to the practice of cutting oneself to identify with the dead (see Lev 19:28; Deut 14:1).

in the house of the ones who love me Possibly a reference to a pagan temple.

13:7 my shepherd Here the metaphor of the shepherd likely represents a royal leader, using the imagery in the sense of Ezekiel and Jeremiah (see Ezek 34:1–10; Jer 23:1–6; compare 1 Kgs 22:17). The shepherd imagery of Zech 10–11 likely included religious leaders such as false prophets (see 11:16–17).

Strike the shepherd The NT quotes this verse in relation to the arrest of Jesus (see Matt 26:31; Mark 14:27).

the sheep may be scattered A common consequence of removing the leader (compare 1 Kgs 22:17; Ezek 34:5–6).

13:9 into the fire Possibly a reference to going into exile.

They are my people A covenant formula expressing the intimate relationship between Yahweh and Israel (see Exod 6:7; Lev 26:12). Hosea uses this formulaic language to describe breaking as well as restoring the relationship (Hos 1:9; 2:23). Variations on the formula appear a number of times in Jeremiah and Ezekiel (e.g., Jer 7:23; 11:4; 24:7; 31:33; Ezek 36:28; 37:27).



 14:1–2 The events described in Zech 14:1–2 correspond to Joel 2:1–11. Jeremiah refers to this period as a time of distress for Jacob (Jer 30:7), whereas Daniel refers to it as the most distressing time since the beginning of nations (Dan 12:1). Each of these passages describes a period of great suffering for the nation of Israel.


14:1 A day is coming for Yahweh A phrase used throughout the prophets to denote the day, when God will intervene in human affairs and judge the wicked (Isa 13:6, 9; Ezek 30:3; Joel 1:15; 2:1; Amos 5:18; Obad 15; Mal 4:5) or bless the righteous (Joel 3:16–21; Obad 17–21; Zeph 3:9–20).

          Day of the Lord HIBD

will be divided in your midst Refers to a conquering army dividing the spoils of war.

14:2 I will gather all the nations Yahweh gathers the nations in order to purify His people (Zech 13:9) and judge their enemies (Joel 3:2; Zech 14:3).

14:3 Yahweh will go forth The depiction of Yahweh as a warrior is a common motif in the OT.

14:4 the Mount of Olives An extended ridgeline bordering Jerusalem to the east. The Mount of Olives is higher in elevation than Jerusalem; it is situated 230 feet above the temple mount.

          Olives, Mount Of HBD

by a very great valley Refers to the Valley of Jehoshaphat—the valley of Yahweh’s judgment (compare Joel 3:2). This great valley will allow the faithful remnant to escape the armies that have captured Jerusalem (Zech 14:5).

14:5 the days of King Uzziah of Judah Also known as Azariah, meaning “Yahweh is my');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, 0, '14:1–2 The events described in Zech 14:1–2 correspond to Joel 2:1–11. Jeremiah refers to this period as a time of distress for Jacob (Jer 30:7), whereas Daniel refers to it as the most distressing time since the beginning of nations (Dan 12:1). Each of these passages describes a period of great suffering for the nation of Israel.


14:1 A day is coming for Yahweh A phrase used throughout the prophets to denote the day, when God will intervene in human affairs and judge the wicked (Isa 13:6, 9; Ezek 30:3; Joel 1:15; 2:1; Amos 5:18; Obad 15; Mal 4:5) or bless the righteous (Joel 3:16–21; Obad 17–21; Zeph 3:9–20).

          Day of the Lord HIBD

will be divided in your midst Refers to a conquering army dividing the spoils of war.

14:2 I will gather all the nations Yahweh gathers the nations in order to purify His people (Zech 13:9) and judge their enemies (Joel 3:2; Zech 14:3).

14:3 Yahweh will go forth The depiction of Yahweh as a warrior is a common motif in the OT.

14:4 the Mount of Olives An extended ridgeline bordering Jerusalem to the east. The Mount of Olives is higher in elevation than Jerusalem; it is situated 230 feet above the temple mount.

          Olives, Mount Of HBD

by a very great valley Refers to the Valley of Jehoshaphat—the valley of Yahweh’s judgment (compare Joel 3:2). This great valley will allow the faithful remnant to escape the armies that have captured Jerusalem (Zech 14:5).

14:5 the days of King Uzziah of Judah Also known as Azariah, meaning “Yahweh is my strength”; reigned ca. 792–742 BC. God blessed Uzziah with a long and prosperous reign because he did right in His sight (2 Kgs 15:3). See note on 2 Chr 26:1.

          Uzziah EDB



14:8 living waters Describes waters flowing rapidly with constant movement and a variety of twists and turns, so that they appear to be something alive. Ezekiel 47:1–12 also describes a river flowing from Jerusalem in the last days.

14:9 Yahweh will be one An allusion drawing attention to the statement of Yahweh’s essential unity in Deut 6:4 (sometimes referred to as the Shema).

14:10 into a desert plateau The rugged terrain south of Jerusalem that is filled with steep hills and deep valleys. It is commonly called the Judaean Wilderness.

from Geba A town given to the Levites located about six miles north of Jerusalem in the territory of Benjamin. The town is to be identified with the modern Jaba.

Rimmon south of Jerusalem A town originally given to the tribe of Simeon located about nine miles north of Beer-sheba. The town is commonly identified with the modern Horvat Rimmon. The name of the town may be related to the Aramean storm god Rimmon.

in its place Each of the subsequent locations is within Jerusalem.

the Gate of Benjamin See note on Jer 20:2. Probably to be identified with the Sheep Gate (see note on Neh 3:1).

the former gate Located on the northern part of the city, possibly the Fish Gate; see note on Neh 3:3.

the Corner Gate Possibly the Valley Gate, which is located near the southwest corner of the city and opens into the Hinnom Valley.

from the Tower of Hananel A tower on the northern side of the city near the temple (Neh 2:8).

the king’s winepresses Probably refers to the king’s garden of Neh 3:15 that is located near the Pool of Siloam at the southeastern corner of the city.

14:12 the plague Alludes to the plagues Yahweh brought against Egypt in Exod 7–11 (compare Zech 14:18).

14:14 the wealth of all the surrounding nations A reversal of the situation of v. 1, where Jerusalem was plundered.

14:16 every survivor The prophets describe how all the nations will one day come to worship Yahweh (compare Isa 2:3; Mic 4:2; Zech 8:20).

the Feast of Booths One of the three feasts during which every male Jew was required to be in Jerusalem (Deut 16:16).

  During this festival, the Jews commemorated the wilderness wanderings of the exodus generation by living in tents set up around Jerusalem. The dedication of Solomon’s temple took place during this feast.

          Feasts HBD

14:18 the clan of Egypt All the nations of the world, including Egypt, will worship Yahweh (see Isa 19:21 and note). They will be subject to judgment for failing to observe');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 1, 'Zechariah is set after the Jewish people had experienced exile in Babylon following the desolation and complete takeover of their land by foreign powers. Zechariah prophesied to a small community of Jews living among the ruins of Jerusalem after returning from exile in Babylon. This community had begun rebuilding the temple on their initial return around 537 bc (Ezra 3:10–12), but the work had stopped when the Jews faced opposition from their neighbors (Ezra 4:4–5). The prophets Haggai and Zechariah encouraged the community to begin rebuilding again (Ezra 5:1–2; Zech 4:9).
     •      The book of Zechariah is commonly divided into two major sections—chs. 1–8 and chs. 9–14. This first section is dominated by a series of eight visions, culminating in the crowning of Joshua, the high priest (6:9–15). The section concludes with a series of four messages addressing proper worship, reflecting on Israel’s past failures, and looking ahead to divine restoration (7:1–8:23). Apocalyptic Literature
           Structure and Themes EBCV8:DMRE


        Introduction to Zechariah

        The Minor Prophets

        How to Study the Bible');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 1, 'eighth month Corresponding to late October and early November. Zechariah’s ministry began about a month before Haggai received his final vision (see Hag 2:10, 20).

        Israelite Calendar Table

the second year of Darius 520 BC. Darius I Hystaspes ruled Persia from 522–486 BC (see note on Hag 1:1). The visions of Zech 1–6 date to the same period as the events in Ezra 4:24–6:13.

          Darius HIBD

the word of Yahweh A standard introductory formula used by the prophets to indicate that they were communicating God’s words, not their own (e.g., Hos 1:1; Joel 1:1; Mic 1:1; Hag 1:1; Zeph 1:1; see note on Isa 1:10).

        Prophetic Commissioning and the Divine Presence

the prophet A prophet is a spokesperson for God (see note on Mic 3:5).

Zechariah A member of one of the priestly families of Judah. His name means “Yahweh remembers.” His grandfather Iddo had returned from the exile with Zerubbabel (Neh 12:1–7). Zechariah eventually became the head of the priestly clan of Iddo (Neh 12:16).

  Although Zechariah was the son of Berechiah (also rendered “Berekiah”) and the grandson of Iddo (Zech 1:1, 7), the book of Ezra indicates he was a descendant of Iddo (Ezra 5:1; 6:14)—likely to indicate that he was a member of that priestly family. Zechariah probably was a young man when he began to prophesy; the Hebrew term used to identify him in Zech 2:4 typically means “boy” or “youth.” Like Jeremiah and Ezekiel, Zechariah was both a prophet and a priest. The writing prophet (and priest) Zechariah must be distinguished from the priest Zechariah (also the son of Berechiah), who was killed by King Joash around 800 BC (2 Chr 24:20–22; compare note on Matt 23:35). Nothing is known of Zechariah’s fate. It is possible that Zech 9–14 was prompted by the unsuccessful invasion of Greece by the Persians in 481–480 BC. If that is the case, the prophet lived at least until this date.

          Zechariah (Person) AYBD

          Zechariah ZEB Q—Z');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 2, 'your ancestors Refers to the pre-exilic generations whose sin against Yahweh brought on His judgment and led to the destruction of Jerusalem and the temple.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 3, 'Yahweh of hosts The title emphasizes Yahweh’s role as commander-in-chief of the heavenly armies, evoking the metaphor of Yahweh as a warrior (see 1 Sam 17:45).

Return The Hebrew term used here conveys the idea of repentance (e.g., Jer 25:5; 35:15; Mal 3:7).

  This command to repent, placed at the beginning of Zechariah, functions as a call to the people to turn to Yahweh and demonstrate their commitment to Him by rebuilding the temple (see Jer 15:19 and note).

          Repentance LTW

          Shub WSOTDICT

        Repentance');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 4, 'the former prophets Refers to the pre-exilic prophets, whose warnings of impending judgment and calls to repentance were routinely ignored.

Return from your evil ways A general description of the words of the prophets (see Isa 31:6; Jer 3:22; Ezek 18:30). Zechariah probably isn’t referring to a specific prophet, but the call to return evokes the essence of Jeremiah’s preaching (see Jer 15:19; 18:11; 25:5; 35:15).

        The Significance of “Shuv” in Jeremiah');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 5, 'Your ancestors, where are they A rhetorical question indicating that the nation’s ancestors had long since passed away.

the prophets, do they live forever This rhetorical question demonstrates that the former prophets have died.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 6, 'my words and my regulations The total body of divine revelation. Refers to the words spoken by prophets and the Law.

And they repented The survivors who had gone into exile repented after experiencing judgment (see Jer 31:18–19).

so he has dealt with us The remnant acknowledged that they deserved their judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 7, 'This passage records eight visions that appear to be in a rough chronological sequence stretching from the time of writing to the coming of the Messiah. The overriding theme of the visions is the expectation of the coming Messiah. This first vision seems to parallel the eighth vision (Zech 6:1–8), as both feature four kinds of horses. The remaining visions also seem to be structured in parallel, in order to present the oracle about the messianic Branch (3:8–10) as the central point of chs. 1–6. The first vision in vv. 7–17 is a pronouncement of hope for the nation. Yahweh promised to put an end to Jerusalem’s 70 years of misery and return the people to the city with comfort and compassion.
   Zechariah may have experienced all eight visions in a single night, but the text never explicitly indicates this. Since a date is given only at v. 7—and because the vision reports are presented in a continuous chain linked by the formula “I lifted my eyes and saw”—it can be inferred that Zechariah saw one vision after another in close successi');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 1, 'The third vision reveals the importance of Jerusalem in God’s restoration program for Israel. Rebuilding the city was a necessary part of the restoration, but this vision points ahead to the ultimate fulfillment of a city protected by Yahweh’s presence (v. 5; compare Rev 21:1–4), not physical walls. The Babylonians’ destruction of the city and temple was caused not by the failure of Jerusalem’s physical defenses, but by the withdrawal of Yahweh’s protective presence (compare Ezek 10:18–19).
   Rebuilding the temple will make way for Yahweh’s presence to return because Yahweh desires a holy place to dwell. The vision addresses the conflicting priorities of the returned exiles: rebuild the temple, or rebuild the city walls in order to protect the temple. Yahweh’s messenger is informing them of the proper priority—rebuilding the temple.
           Zechariah 2:1–13 TOTC HagZecMal');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 1, 'a man, and in his hand was a measuring rope This figure with a measuring line is likely in the background of Rev 11:1, which draws on imagery from Zechariah (see Zech 1:16) and Ezekiel (see Ezek 40:3 and note).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 2, 'what is its length The man measures Jerusalem to prepare to build a wall around the city. He seems to be planning to measure Jerusalem’s former size to rebuild the ruined wall, unaware of Yahweh’s plan for an expanded Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 4, 'Run The second angel dispatches the first to reveal to the young man that his plan is not necessary for God’s purpose.

young man This Hebrew term typically “boy” or “youth.” The characterization may serve to emphasize his youthful ignorance; he has not yet learned that Yahweh’s plans are not limited by human perceptions of what is possible.

like villages without walls The population of Jerusalem will be so great that it cannot be contained by a walled city. An unwalled city also symbolized openness to all who would come (see Zech 8:20–22; compare Ezek 38:11).

  The image points to a future fulfillment when all Israel and all the nations are gathered under Yahweh’s rule, a prominent theme throughout the Prophets. Compare Zech 2:11; Isa 2:2–3; 11:12; 43:5–6; 49:16–21; 54:2–3; 60:3–14; 66:18; Jer 3:17; 23:3; 29:14; 31:8–11; 32:37; Ezek 11:17; 28:25–26; 34:13–14; Hos 1:11; Mic 2:12–13.

          Zechariah 2:4 TOTC HagZecMal');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 5, 'a wall of fire all around An unwalled city was vulnerable to attack (Ezek 38:11). This lack of visible, physical protection likely motivated the exiles (represented by the man with the measuring line) to make preparations for the protection of the city. The message revealed to Zechariah is that Jerusalem does not need walls because Yahweh Himself will protect it when His presence returns to the city (see note on Zech 2:1–13).

I will be the glory in its midst Refers to the glory of Yahweh that inhabited the tabernacle and temple (Exod 40:35; 1 Kgs 8:11).

  In Isaiah, God’s glory among His people often symbolized salvation and restoration (see Isa 4:5; 24:23; 60:18–19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 6, 'The third vision is followed by prophetic oracles emphasizing themes from the earlier visions: the restoration of Israel and judgment on the nations. The oracle in Zech 2:6–9 calls for Jewish exiles still living among the nations to return to Zion before Yahweh judges those nations. The oracle in vv. 10–12 emphasizes Yahweh’s intention to return to Zion and gather Israel and the nations to worship together. The section ends by affirming that Yahweh has set His plan in motion to fulfill the promises of salvation and restoration (v. 13).
           Zechariah 2:6–13 TOTC HagZecMal');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 6, 'Flee from Isaiah and Jeremiah also commanded the nation to return to Judah following the exile (e.g., Isa 48:20; Jer 31:16–21). See note on Ezra 1:3.

the land of the north May refer to Babylon, but this phrase probably is used figuratively for the foreign lands where Jewish exiles were scattered.

  Jeremiah refers to the north as the direction from which both invasion and returning exiles would come (Jer 3:18; 6:22; 10:22; 16:15; 31:8). The reference to the four winds here suggests the prophet’s appeal is directed at all Jewish exiles in all lands where they have settled (compare Jer 23:8). Isaiah also depicts the gathering of God’s people from all directions (Isa 43:5–6; 49:12).

          North DBI

          North EDB

the four winds of the heavens Referring to the four directions on the compass or indicating that the exiles are spread out in the same way as Yahweh’s messengers who cover the entire world in their patrols (compare Zech 6:5–7; Dan 7:2). See note on Zech 6:5.

          Four DBI');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 8, 'nations plundering you A reference to the second vision (see 1:19).

the apple of his eye Refers to the opening of the eye—the pupil. The prophet expresses that harming Israel is equal to harming the most vulnerable and sensitive part of Yahweh Himself. Compare Deut 32:10.

  The term babah occurs only here in the Bible. It is a cognate of the Akkadian term babu, meaning “gate.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 10, 'O daughter of Zion A metaphor for the inhabitants of Jerusalem. See note on Isa 1:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 11, 'on that day Refers to the day that Yahweh returns to Jerusalem.

they will be my people The Israelites were the original people of Yahweh, the nation He redeemed from Egypt (see Exod 3:7; 5:1; 6:7). This verse proclaims that foreigners have the same opportunity to become the people of God. See note on Jer 30:22; note on Ezek 11:20; compare Zech 8:7–8.

  The covenant formula (Lev 26:12) that had reflected Israel’s special relationship with Yahweh will one day be extended to include all nations. Prophetic oracles of restoration often hinted at Yahweh’s plan for future rule of the nations where the nations would also worship Yahweh in Jerusalem (e.g., Isa 56:6–7). See Isa 2:2–4; note on Zech 2:2.

          Zechariah 2:11 Zechariah (NAC)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 12, 'will inherit The Hebrew verb used here commonly appears in legal texts of the OT in reference to a property acquisition or an inheritance. See note on Ezek 36:12.

          Nachal WSOTDICT

his portion Refers to an inheritance.

the holy land This is the only occurrence of the Hebrew phrase used here in the OT, but the same concept of sacred space appears elsewhere in the Hebrew text with designations such as “holy ground” (Exod 3:5), “holy mountain” (Isa 11:9), or “holy place” (Ezra 9:8).

          Sacred Space LTW');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 13, 'Be silent, all people, before Yahweh This verse is an allusion (or indirect refe');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 1, 'Zechariah’s fourth vision describes the nation’s cleansing from sin and the reinstatement of its priestly office and functions (compare Exod 19:6). The vision reestablishes the authority of the Zadokite priesthood and addresses some of the criticisms toward it in the last part of Isaiah (which also seems to address the situation of the early postexilic community; compare Isa 57:1–10; 58:1–5). Zechariah’s vision emphasizes the purification of the high priest, allowing him to properly represent the people before Yahweh.
           Zechariah 3:1–7 NM');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 1, 'Joshua Refers to Joshua son of Jehozadak (also rendered “Jozadak”), the first high priest of the remnant that returned from exile.

  The Hebrew name for Joshua, yehoshua'', means “Yahweh is salvation” or “Yahweh saves.” A shortened spelling is used in Ezra and Nehemiah (see Ezra 3:2). The name “Jesus” derives from this name.

          Jeshua HIBD



  Joshua son of Jehozadak
 As the first high priest of the returned remnant, Joshua reestablished proper worship of Yahweh by building an altar and resuming sacrificial worship (ca. 537 BC; Ezra 3:1–6). He is identified elsewhere as the son of Jehozadak (or “Jozadak”; Hag 1:1; Ezra 3:2). His first name is sometimes spelled as “Jeshua” (e.g., Ezra 3:2 uses yeshua in Hebrew, not yehoshua as in Zech 3:1). His father was among the priests taken into exile after the fall of Jerusalem in 586 BC (1 Chr 6:15). His grandfather, Seraiah, who had been high priest when Jerusalem fell, was put to death by Nebuchadnezzar (2 Kgs 25:18–21; 1 Chr 6:14). Joshua plays an important role when the prophets Haggai and Zechariah remind the exiles of their need to finish rebuilding the temple (Ezra 5:1–2; Hag 1:1–15).


high priest Joshua was of the priestly family of Jedaiah, one of the 24 priestly families that David organized (1 Chr 24:7).

the angel of Yahweh Representing Yahweh Himself in the vision (see note on Zech 1:11). Since the Angel of Yahweh can either be a representative of Yahweh or the embodiment of Yahweh on earth, the scene here could include either three or four figures: the Angel of Yahweh, Satan, and the priest, or those three plus Yahweh Himself.

Satan Literally “the adversary.” See note on Job 1:6.

        Satan in the Old Testament and the Serpent of Genesis 3

  accuse him The verbal form of the term satan means “to act as an adversary.” Satan’s accusation likely centered on the high priest’s ritual impurity (see Zech 3:3). Leviticus 22:3 commanded that an unclean priest was to be cut off (see note on Lev 7:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 2, 'a stick A stick used for stirring fires. Illustrates that God graciously rescued Joshua (and the rest of the remnant) just as he was about to be destroyed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 3, 'filthy garments Describes garments stained with excrement, representing Joshua’s sinfulness and ritual impurity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 4, 'the ones standing before him Probably refers to other angels, though human associates of the priest are possible.

will clothe you with rich garments The priests were to wear specially consecrated garments (see Lev 8:7–13, 30; 16:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 5, 'a clean headband A headdress formed by wrapping long strips of cloth around the head. The turban was part of the priest’s uniform (Lev 8:9).

          Turban EDB');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 7, 'you will walk in my ways A common metaphor for obedience. Joshua’s reinstatement is conditional. He must keep Yahweh’s laws in order to keep his position of leadership (Exod 18:20; Deut 5:33; 10:12). Compare the change in leadership over a similar failure in 1 Sam 8:1–5.

you will judge Denotes the act of judging. The priests’ duties also extended to deciding legal issues (Deut 17:9). Their decisions involved maintaining the ritual purity of the temple (compare Ezek 44:23–24).

passageways The high priest was the only person authorized to enter the holiest part of the temple (here, “my house” and “my courts”). This access was permitted once a year on the Day of Atonement (Lev 16:29–33).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 8, 'This oracle expands on the vision in Zech 3:1–7 by placing the leadership of Joshua (see note on v. 1) within the context of Yahweh’s full plan of restoration, culminating in the coming of the Messiah. The imagery in v. 8 connects the messianic expectations of Isa 4:2–6 with the Suffering Servant figure from Isa 42–53.
           Zechariah 3:8–10 NM');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 8, 'your companions that are sitting before you These friends are probably distinct from the standing ones of Zech 3:4, 7. They likely are associates of the priest, perhaps even priests themselves.

a sign The Hebrew word used here can refer to a miraculous sign (Exod 4:21; 11:9), a celestial omen (Joel 2:30), or a symbolic event, person, or action (Isa 8:18; 20:3; Ez');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 1, 'Zechariah’s fifth vision is a symbolic picture of the Spirit of Yahweh as He gives power to the anointed ones currently in view—probably Zerubbabel and Joshua. This vision has some similarities to the fourth vision, including a reference to seven eyes, a reference to a stone for the temple, and an emphasis on a historical person.
   In this vision, everything Zechariah sees holds significance. The vision is presented almost as if it were a dream, with an angelic guide explaining the fantastic imagery (compare Dan 8:18; 10:9–10). Zechariah is shown an unusual lampstand—one that is different from a typical menorah or temple lampstand. Zechariah asks three times about it and the two olive trees next to it. In the cryptic fashion typical of apocalyptic visions (compare Dan 7:15–28), the angel’s explanation offers little clarity, emphasizing simply that Yahweh has chosen Zerubbabel for this mission to rebuild the temple and that Yahweh Himself will overcome the worldly obstacles to its reconstruction.
           Zechariah 4:1–14 NM
           Zechariah 4:1–14 TOTC HagZecMal');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 2, 'a lampstand all of gold Describes a column that supported one or more oil lamps. The lampstands mentioned in the OT were almost always made of gold and designed to be used in religious worship settings (compare 2 Kgs 4:10).

  This lampstand consisted of a large bowl to hold the oil with seven smaller receptacles around the rim of the large bowl; each smaller receptacle had seven notches to hold the wicks for the seven lights. The entire lampstand would have had 49 lights.

          Zechariah 4:2 TOTC HagZecMal

          Zechariah 4:2 ZIBBC OT 5

          Lampstand EDB

a bowl A receptacle for the olive oil.

seven lips on each of Refers to spouts or pipes. Bowls used for lamps were often pinched seven times around the rim to form a notch where the wick could be held.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 3, 'two olive trees Ancients used olive oil as fuel for lamps. The surprising thing about this lampstand was that it was fed with oil directly from the trees through the golden pipes (Zech 4:12). The olive, as food and fuel, was an important part of the ancient Near Eastern economy.

          Olive EDB');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 6, 'Zerubbabel A descendant of King David (1 Chr 3:17–19; Matt 1:12), as well as the governor of Judah (Hag 1:1) who oversaw the rebuilding of the temple (Ezra 3:2, 8; 5:2; see note on Ezra 3:2).

my Spirit Emphasizing that divine power—not human power—will enable them to overcome the opposition to rebuilding the temple. Throughout the OT, God’s Spirit gives divine empowerment to the individuals God selects (e.g., Exod 31:3; Num 11:25–26; 24:2; Judg 3:10; 6:34; 11:29; 1 Sam 10:10; 16:13).

          Spirit EDB');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 7, 'O great mountain A metaphor for a difficult task. The mountain symbolizes all the challenges that the community will face as they attempt to complete the work that God commanded. The vision serves to reassure them that Yahweh is on their side and will help them overcome the obstacles.

Before Zerubbabel you will become level ground Indicates that Zerubbabel, through the power of God’s Spirit (Zech 4:6), will successfully complete the difficult task Yahweh gave to him.

  In Matthew’s Gospel, Jesus notes the ability of Yahweh to move a mountain, showing God’s power to do the impossible for those who have faith (Matt 17:20).

the top stone Represents the final stone of the temple that Zerubbabel will build (Zech 4:9).

Grace, grace Acknowledges that Yahweh’s Spirit—not human might—was responsible for completing the temple project (v. 6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 9, 'The hands of Zerubbabel The initial effort to rebuild the temple was organized by Zerubbabel (Ezra 3:8–13). He had helped start the work by personally rebuilding the altar alongside the high priest Joshua and the other priests (see Ezra 3:1–3).

his hands will finish it Under the leadership of Zerubbabel, the remnant would continue to work on the temple for the next four years. They completed the project on the third day of Adar in the sixth year of Darius (March 12, 515 BC; see Ezra 6:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 10, 'plumb line The Hebrew term used here seems to refer to a string with a weight attached to the end. Ancients used tools like plumb lines to determine whether a wall was straight.

  If a wall was leaning, it was more likely to collapse unless it was repaired. The image connotes destruction and reconstruction. If the wall was too damaged to repair, it would be demolished and rebuilt. Compare Amos 7:7 and note.

          Plumb Line EDB

the eyes of Yahweh Symbolizing Yahweh’s awareness of all things. See note on Zech 3:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 11, 'these two olive trees Zechariah has to ask twice (v. 12) for clarification on the significance of the two trees. See note on v. 3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 12, 'these two twigs of The Hebrew term here, meaning “ears,” is usually used in the sense of ears of grain (Gen 41:5).

golden pipes Allowed the olive oil to run from the trees into the bowl reservoir.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 14, 'the two anointed ones The Hebrew text here reads “sons of fresh oil.” The Israelites commonly used olive oil for anointing (Exod 29:7; 1 Sam 16:12–13), which served to set an individual apart for a special purpose.

  Only two types of officials were anointed in the OT: kings and high priests. The “anointed ones” here are probably Zerubbabel and Joshua, the representatives of David and Aaron in this renewed covenant community. Compare note on Zech 6:11.

          Anoint HBD');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 1, 'Zechariah’s sixth vision illustrates Yahweh’s judgment or curse against all who have sinned by violating His commandments. Just as the fourth vision dealt with the cleansing of the priest’s sin (see 3:1–5), this imagery is likely related to the need for all to be cleansed from their sins brought on by breaking Yahweh’s covenant. The vision addresses the sins of social injustice and religious apostasy. Sin must be dealt with before Yahweh can return and dwell among His people (2:10).
           Zechariah 5:1–4 NM');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 1, 'a flying scroll This scroll descends from God’s presence in heaven. The written word plays an important role in prophetic and apocalyptic vision imagery (e.g., Ezek 2:8–3:4; Hab 2:2; Isa 8:1; Dan 5:7–25; Rev 5:1–5; compare note on Ezek 2:8).

          Writing HIBD

          Scroll DBI');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 2, 'twenty cubits long Approximately 30–34 feet. A normal cubit was about 18 inches. A “royal” or “great” cubit was about 21 inches.

ten cubits wide About 15–17 feet. The length of a scroll would usually be many times its width, measured from top to bottom. This heavenly scroll is of immense size. A typical scroll might be 30–35 feet long unrolled, but the writing surface was usually only about 10–12 inches wide.

  Zechariah either sees an unrolled document (written on both sides) that is roughly 15 by 30 feet in size, or a rolled scroll with a writing length of 30 feet (compared to the usual 12 inches) and a rolled diameter of 15 feet. The latter would provide a much greater overall writing surface. A scroll with writing on both sides was likely made from papyrus, not parchment. See note on Ezek 2:10.

          Paper, Papyrus HIBD');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 3, 'curse The Hebrew term used here alludes to the punishments associated with breaking the covenant (see Deut 29:10–29; Ezek 16:59).

  A formal ancient Near Eastern covenant or treaty would have the consequences for breaking the treaty spelled out in detail in a section toward the end of the document. The curse, therefore, has a judicial or legal function, encouraging the parties to keep their part of the agreement. This scroll appears to contain only the curses or punishments for breaking the law, similar to Ezekiel’s scroll in Ezek 2:10.

          Curse DDD

          Curse TLOT

everyone who steals Compare Exod 20:15.

everyone who swears Refers to misusing the name of Yahweh (see Exod 20:7).

on the other side A scroll with writing on both sides was uncommon (see note on Ezek 2:10). The two sides of the scroll reflect the two main types of sin: sin against a neighbor (or another person) and sin against God.

  The Ten Commandments (Exod 20:1–17) break down along two major categories: laws governing the people’s relationship with Yahweh and laws governing their relationships with each other. One side of the scroll in Zechariah’s vision contained a curse against misusing the name of God. The other side contained a curse against all who steal. Since each is the middle command (the third and eighth) of these two parts of the Ten Commandments, they are probably meant to represent the entire Law.

          Ten Commandments EDB');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 4, 'it will spend the night in that house It will remain until its intended mission is accomplished. This curse will be in effect until the land is cleansed of sin.

and will destroy it, with its timber and its stone Probably a reference to an individual’s life; God will strike down those who violate His commands.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 5, 'The seventh vision presents Zechariah with the strange sight of a woman imprisoned in a barrel or basket. The woman personifies wickedness and is about to be taken to the land of Babylon. The passage reveals that the land of Babylon will become a center of evil when the woman is established there as an idol for those who worship evil.
   The personification of evil or wickedness in female form has parallels with the image of the forbidden woman in Prov 7:5–27 and the great prostitute of Rev 17. The imagery focuses on evil as a power in and of itself—an external, objective force, not merely the sinful actions of people. God alone has supreme power over evil, directing His angels to remove it to distant Shinar (also known as Babylon or Babylonia), a location symbolic of rebellious opposition to Yahweh (Gen 11:2; compare Rev 14:8).
           Zechariah 5:5–11 NM');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 6, 'a basket The Hebrew word ephah used here is a technical term for a unit used to measure volume (both dry and liquid). It often denotes “basket” throughout Zech 5:6–11. Estimates of the capacity vary from around 0.8 cubic feet to 1.25 cubic feet (22 to 36 liters).

  Identifying the capacity of the basket in this passage is puzzling because it is too small for a container holding a woman (v. 7). It is possible that this passage does not indicate the unit of measure but relates to Mesopotamian religion. One proposal based on possible Semitic cognates is that ephah refers to the small room or shrine that sat on top of a ziggurat. The destination of the basket as described in v. 11 favors this possibility. On the other hand, a prophetic vision could include larger-than-life elements (like the flying scroll of v. 2 that measured at least 30 feet by 15 feet). Thus, the size of what Zechariah saw relative to the size of a typical ephah may be irrelevant.

their iniquity The Hebrew word here literally means “their eye.”

  Many English translations follow the Greek and Syriac translations of the OT, which appear to have read “their iniquity.” The difference in spelling is only one letter in ancient Hebrew, and the two letters in question (vav and yod) looked very similar in handwritten texts. The meaning “iniquity” makes sense in light of the description of the basket’s contents as “wickedness” in v. 8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 7, 'a woman The representation of evil as a woman being carried to Babylon likely lies behind the symbolism of Rev 14:8. See note on Zech 5:5–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 8, 'Wickedness The term used here refers to evil in general (Deut 9:4–5; Prov 11:5; Ezek 18:27; Mal 3:18).

the lead cover Since lead is the heaviest of all common metals, its great weight causes the basket to become a cage or prison for the woman (who is personified wickedness).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 9, 'the wind The Hebrew term here may be used with the connotation of “spirit,” indicating that God’s Spirit was empowering these women (compare Zech 4:6).

their wings Indicates the women are likely heavenly beings.

a stork A large, wading bird that has been noted from ancient times as a bird that takes affectionate care of its offspring. The stork was a high-flying bird.

  The idea that the stork is affectionate is reflected in the stork’s name; the word chasidah, meaning “stork,” comes from the word chasid, meaning “loving,” or “faithful.” Storks were unclean for consumption according to Lev 11:13, 19.

          Stork HBD');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 11, 'Shinar This land was known variously as “Babylon,” “Babylonia,” or “Shinar” (Gen 10:10; see note on Ezra 1:11).

  The Bible, at times, uses Babylon as a symbol for the center of humankind’s wickedness (e.g., Gen 11:1–9; Isa 13–14; Jer 50–51). Babylon eventually will be depicted as the center of Satan’s earthly kingdom (Rev 17–18).

          Shinar EDB');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 1, 'The eighth and final vision illustrates the universal reign of Yahweh. This vision parallels the opening vision (Zech 1:7–17): Both feature the number four, horses of various colors, a valley—all with an emphasis on the entire earth. The two visions function as an inclusio, bracketing the sequence of visions in chs. 1–6.
           Zechariah 6:1–8 NM
           Zechariah 6:1–15 TOTC HagZecMal
           Zechariah 6:1–8 Zechariah (NAC)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 1, 'four chariots Compare with the four horses of 1:8. Each chariot was probably pulled by two horses. See note on Nah 2:3.

          Chariots HIBD

between The chariots progress through a valley, probably symbolic of the entrance of Yahweh’s abode. The chariots representing His divine power are sent out from His holy mountain.

two mountains The two bronze mountains function symbolically like the two bronze pillars framing the entrance to the temple (compare 1 Kgs 7:15–22).

  The symbolism of the two mountains also might parallel ancient Near Eastern imagery of a sun god rising between the mountains. The bronze color represented the sunlight reflecting on the mountains. There may be a connection to Zech 14:4, which describes how two mountains are created when Yahweh stands on the Mount of Olives and it splits in two.

          Zechariah 6:1 TOTC HagZecMal

          Zechariah 6:1 ZIBBC OT 5

bronze This metal alloy made from the combination of copper and tin was commonly used in fortifications and could reinforce gateways (Jer 1:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 3, 'strong dappled horses The term used here could also refer to the degree to which the horses of the fourth chariot were spotted—they were “strongly dappled.” The variety of colored horses compares with the vision of Zech 1:8 and likely influenced the imagery in Rev 6:2–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 5, 'the four winds of the heavens The Hebrew term ruach is likely used here to denote “spirit” rather than “wind” (see note on Ezek 37:5; compare Ezek 37:9). Some translations phrase this passage to imply the chariots are being sent out to the four winds—that is, in all four directions. The underlying Hebrew phrase, however, clearly equates the chariots with the winds.

  The number four represents the whole of the created order and often appears in depictions of divine judgment. The four chariots represent the four winds (or “spirits”) of heaven, Yahweh’s messengers of judgment (compare Jer 49:36; Dan 7:2; Psa 104:4). Zechariah asks, “What are these?” in Zech 6:4, referring to the chariots he saw in vv. 1–3. The angel answers, “These are the four winds of heaven going forth” (v. 5). Another difficulty with equating the winds with four directions comes in v. 6, where the chariots are sent in only two directions—north and south.

          Four DBI

          Wind DBI

presenting themselves before the Lord of Probably in the sense of standing before the divine council (or heavenly court) for commissioning. The same verb form is used in Job 1:6 describing the “sons of God” gathering before Yahweh. See note on Job 1:6. Compare Exod 19:17; Num 11:16; Deut 31:14; Judg 20:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 6, 'the north country Symbolic of the location of Yahweh’s enemies throughout much of the OT (compare Zech 2:6 and note). The greater importance of the north as symbolic of evil is indicated by the two chariots heading that direction; in comparison, only one heads south.

  Jeremiah describes divine judgment coming from the north (Jer 1:14 and note). Ezekiel also emphasizes the northern county as the home of Israel’s eschatological enemy who will invade at the end of days (Ezek 38:14–16). The imagery probably developed out of the reality that Israel was usually subject to catastrophic invasion from the north by different Mesopotamian powers (e.g., Babylon, Assyria). The Canaanite god Baal was also depicted living on a mountain in the north (see note on Isa 2:2). North also indicated the left side, based on the eastward orientation common to the ancient Near East (see Gen 14:15; Ezek 16:46). In Semitic cultures, the left side is associated with calamity. These factors made the north country symbolic of all that was evil and sinister in the world. The end of the vision in Zech 6:8 indicates that these divine messengers subdued the evil power of the north, making the world ready for Yahweh’s presence to return.

          North DBI

the south country Possibly refers to Egypt. Israel’s location, with the Mediterranean to the west and the desert to the east, meant that large-scale invasion only came from the south (Egypt) or the north. The most destructive invasions came from the north. The opposing powers of north and south play an important role in the vision of Dan 11:2–45.

  The south can represent both positive and hostile elements. As the opposite of the hostile “north,” it can be construed in a favorable way. Yahweh is also depicted as dwelling in the south or coming to Israel out of the south, connected to the southern mountains such as Mount Sinai, Paran, Teman, or Seir (Deut 33:2; Judg 5:4–5; Hab 3:3). The south was also associated with the inhospitable desert of the Negev and, thus, could be used as a symbol of judgment (Zech 9:14; Isa 21:1; 30:6; Job 37:17). The country of Egypt also represented a traditional enemy of Israel, the site of their former slavery and oppression (Deut 5:6, 15).

          South DBI

          Egypt DBI');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 7, 'patrol Compare Zech 1:10–11, where God’s messengers had just returned from patrolling the earth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 9, 'This oracle serves as a climax to the eight night visions and reiterates Yahweh’s choice of Joshua as high priest (see note on 3:1–7). Zechariah is commanded to make a crown for Joshua out of silver and gold. This symbolic act establishes the priests as the divinely appointed leaders of the community. The emphasis on the temple and religious leadership reflects the realistic situation of the exiles, who had more freedom over religious matters than political affairs. The passage also serves to connect the visions of chs. 1–6 with the oracles of chs. 7–8.
           Zechariah 6:9–15 NM


        Symbolic Actions of the Prophets Table');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 9, 'the word of Yahweh Identifying this passage as a prophetic oracle. See note on 1:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 10, 'from the exiles Many Jews remained in Babylon instead of returning to the land of Israel (see note on Ezra 1:3), so there were new returnees joining the community for some time (see Ezra 4:12 and note). These three men are possibly the leaders or at least the most prominent, wealthy members of a group that recently arrived from Babylon. They provide the precious metal that will be used to fashion a crown for Joshua, suggesting they were wealthy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 11, 'a crown A headpiece signifying royalty or a position of honor. The priest’s headdress is described in Exod 29:6; 39:30.

  The word used here is the usual term for a royal crown (2 Sam 12:30; Psa 21:3; Jer 13:18). It is otherwise never used for the headdress of a priest. The terminology here serves to subtly link the high priest with royal authority. The word is grammatically plural, raising questions over how many crowns were made. See note on Zech 6:12.

          Zechariah 6:11 Zechariah (NAC)

          Crown HBD

          Crown DBI

set it on the head of Joshua The identification of the messianic Branch with a priest (v. 12) foreshadows the uniting of kingship and priesthood in the person of the Messiah (compare Psa 110:2, 4).

  This passage probably inspired expectations of a priestly Messiah in Second Temple Judaism. The group responsible for the sectarian Dead Sea Scrolls expected a priestly messiah in addition to a prophet like Moses and a royal Davidic messiah (see the documents Rule of the Community 9.11; the Rule of the Congregation 2.12–22; and the Melchizedek Scroll). The author of Hebrews makes a similar connection when proclaiming Jesus’ high priesthood (Heb 2:17–18; compare note on John 1:21). For more on Joshua, see note on Zech 3:1.

          Two Messiahs AI:IJAL

          Beliefs of the Qumran Sect BEB');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 12, 'Branch See note on 3:8.

he will build the temple of Yahweh Refers to the temple in Jerusalem. Zechariah’s vision in 4:1–14 had identified Zerubbabel as the one who would successfully complete the temple rebuilding project (see 4:9), but he is not directly in view in this prophecy. Instead, it is Joshua the high priest who is emphasized.

  The intertwining themes of priestly and royal authority could be more appropriately applied to the two separate figures of Joshua and Zerubbabel (see note on 4:14). Zerubbabel may be in view in the vision, as the Branch, since the two leaders are almost always mentioned together in Ezra and Nehemiah (see note on 3:1). According to this interpretation, the crown from v. 11 may refer to two or more crowns. The use of two precious metals—silver and gold—could also point to the existence of two crowns. However, Zerubbabel never rules a politically independent nation, which the text seems to imply as necessary. It could be that a completely different figure is in view.

  The ultimate fulfillment of this imagery comes when Jesus, from the line of David, unites the roles of priest and king to truly establish Yahweh’s kingdom on earth (Heb 3:3; Eph 2:20–22).

          Zechariah 6:12 Zechariah (NAC)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 13, 'will build the temple of Yahweh The repetition of this phrase from the end of');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 7, 1, 'This passage addresses the practical implications of Judah’s renewed faith in Yahweh. The occasion of the oracles in Zech 7:4–14 is the inquiry of a delegation from Bethel regarding the observance of fast days commemorating the siege and destruction of Jerusalem and the temple (vv. 1–3). Zechariah’s first message criticizes these fasts as inappropriate to the new beginning symbolized by the reestablished Jewish community (vv. 4–7). The second message emphasizes the importance of ethical living over empty religious observances (vv. 8–14).
           Zechariah 7:1–14 NM');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 7, 1, 'in the fourth year of King Darius Refers to 518 BC. This final chronological heading in Zechariah indicates that about two years had passed since he delivered his call to repentance (see 1:1 and note) and experienced the eight visions (see 1:7–6:8 and note).





Kislev The ninth month in the Hebrew calendar; aligns roughly to late November and early December. This date formula places these messages in early December, 518 BC.

        Israelite Calendar Table');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 7, 2, 'the people of Bethel A city about 10 miles north of Jerusalem, near the southern border of the former northern kingdom of Israel.

          Bethel EDB

          Bethel ISBE



  Bethel
 Bethel, which means “house of God,” was an ancient sacred site (see Gen 28:10–22) that was associated with improper worship during the time of the northern kingdom (1 Kgs 12:28–33; Amos 3:14; 4:4; 7:13). Jeroboam, the first king of the northern kingdom, had established Israelite religious centers at Bethel in the southern part of his kingdom and at Dan in the north (1 Kgs 12:28–29). The purpose of the shrine was to discourage people from making a pilgrimage to Jerusalem for worship. Biblical writers routinely criticized the worship at Bethel as improper idolatry, lamenting that the kings of Israel continued this practice begun by Jeroboam (see 1 Kgs 16:25–26; Amos 4:4; 7:7–13).


Sharezer An Assyrian name meaning “protect the king” (2 Kgs 19:37; Isa 37:38).

Regem-Melech Means “friend of the king.”

to entreat the favor of Yahweh This group from Bethel is sent to offer sacrifices at the temple in Jerusalem. The remnant had set up the altar immediately after returning (Ezra 3:1–3) following the decree of Cyrus (Ezra 1:2–4). At this time, they were rebuilding the temple (Ezra 3:10–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 7, 3, 'Should I mourn The delegation from Bethel was seeking the endorsement of the religious leaders in Jerusalem for their religious observance.

and keep myself separate The Jews had imposed a religious observance commemorating Nebuchadnezzar’s destruction of Jerusalem and the temple in 586 BC (2 Kgs 25:8–9); this involved fasting (see Zech 7:5).

the fifth month The month of Ab, late July and early August.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 7, 5, 'seventh Tishri—late September and early October. This observance may have commemorated the murder of Gedaliah, the governor Nebuchadnezzar placed over Judah (2 Kgs 25:22–25). This event precipitated the remnant’s flight to Egypt (2 Kgs 25:26).

seventy years Referring to the time of exile. See note on Jer 25:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 7, 6, 'drinking for yourselves The religious observance of the inhabitants of Bethel was a matter of posturing rather than piety. They were observing the fast simply to draw attention to themselves instead of offering true repentance to Yahweh.

  Their commemorative observance had become a legalistic ritual instead of an act of heartfelt devotion toward God. They were also mourning the loss of their possessions and their great city instead of their lost relationship with God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 7, 7, 'former prophets Refers to prophets such as Isaiah, Jeremiah, and Micah.

the Negev The desert region south of Jerusalem was called the Negev, meaning “south.”

the Shephelah Jerusalem was located at the top of a mountain; the region to the west of the city was often called the lowland as it was much lower in elevation. This region included the Shephelah and the Philistine plain.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 7, 9, 'with trustworthy justice The prophets continually emphasize that the ethical aspects of God’s law are just as important as the ritual aspects (see Isa 1:17 and note; compare Mic 6:8; Matt 23:23).

steadfast love The Hebrew term used here, chesed, is often used in reference to');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 1, 'This chapter contains Zechariah’s final two explanatory messages (see note on Zech 7:1–14). Zechariah’s third message is one of restoration (vv. 1–17). The fourth message is one of rejoicing (vv. 18–23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 1, 'Yahweh of hosts See note on Nah 2:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 2, 'Zion Another name for Jerusalem. See note on Isa 1:8.

I am jealous for it with great wrath Yahweh is fiercely protective of His people. All nations who come against Israel will experience the wrath of God (see Gen 12:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 3, 'I will dwell in the midst of Jerusalem A reference to the messianic reign (Psa 2:6), when Yahweh Himself will reign over the entire earth from Jerusalem (Joel 3:16–17; Zech 14:9; see note on Zeph 1:4).

the holy mountain Jerusalem is situated at the top of a mountain. Compare Isaiah 2:2–3; Psa 48:1 and note.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 4, 'Old men and old women Symbolizes the peaceful state of the city.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 5, 'boys and girls Creates a merism with the old men and women of Zech 8:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 6, 'it seems impossible to the remnant of Zechariah depicts his audience as skeptical of this kind of transformation. At this time, Jerusalem was largely unpopulated and full of ruins (see Neh 7:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 7, 'my people Refers to the Israelites, whom Yahweh redeemed from Egypt (Exod 3:7, 10; 5:1; 6:7; 7:4, 16; 8:1).

the land of the east, and from the land of the west Represents of all nations on the earth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 8, 'they will dwell in the midst of Jerusalem The gathering of the Jews to the land of Israel is a common theme in the prophets (e.g., Ezek 20:33–44; Amos 9:14–15; Zeph 3:20).

They will be my people Compare Hosea 2:23.

in faithfulness and in righteousness This situation will be the opposite of Isa 48:1, which uses the same words.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 9, 'your hands This phrase forms an inclusio (a bracketing by repetition) with the end of Zech 8:13.

in these days Refers to Zechariah’s present time.

the mouth of the prophets Includes Haggai and Zechariah, who were instrumental in encouraging the Jewish people to restart the rebuilding of the temple in 520 BC (see Ezra 5:1–2). The Jewish people halted the rebuilding project shortly after rebuilding the altar in 536 BC, because they feared their enemies (Ezra 4:1–5).



the house of Yahweh of The temple in Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 10, 'those days Refers to the days when the temple project was restarted. The rebuilding of the temple resumed in 520 BC after a period of inactivity (compare note on Zech 8:9).

wage for people or for animals Refers to the economic distress that took place while the temple lay in ruins (see Hag 1:5–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 11, 'the remnant of this people Referring to the returnees. See note on Isa 1:9; compare note on Jer 24:8.

the former days Refers to the days prior to the commencement of the rebuilding project when the temple lay in ruins.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 12, 'a sowing of peace Yahweh will allow the produce of the ground to grow without destroying it (see Hag 1:10–11).

the vine will give its fruit Refers to a grapevine and grapes. Compare Haggai 1:11.

          Vine HBD

the soil will give its produce A reference to grain, probably wheat and barley. Compare Haggai 1:10.

the heavens will give their dew Compare Haggai 1:10.

I will cause the remnant of this people to inherit all these things Indicates that Yahweh will give the Israelites bountiful harvests.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 13, 'O house of Judah and house of Israel A collective reference to the Israelites of the northern and southern kingdoms.

let your hands be strong See note on Zech 8');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 1, 'This oracle proclaims Yahweh’s judgment against Israel’s neighbors (Zech 9:1–8) and emphasizes His concern to protect His people (vv. 11–17). The list of cities in vv. 1–7 proceeds generally from north to south, and v. 8 depicts Yahweh’s return to His temple in Jerusalem. The passage ends with Yahweh establishing complete peace and security (vv. 11–17). Two verses in the middle of the chapter announce the return of a king to Zion who takes his place to rule over a now-pacified world (vv. 9–10). This king’s arrival brings the ultimate peace over all the earth that will follow Yahweh’s total restoration of His rule (Isa 2:4; Hos 2:18). The entire oracle reflects the imagery of the Divine Warrior battling on behalf of His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 1, 'An oracle The Hebrew term used here (massa) can refer both to a burden (e.g., Exod 23:5; Deut 1:12) and oracle or prophecy (e.g., Ezek 12:10; Isa 13:1; Hab 1:1). It is frequently used to identify prophetic oracles (Nah 1:1; Isa 15:1; 17:1; 19:1). The prophets may use this word ironically, referring to the oracle as a burden that they must carry or implying that the message is a burden for the audience to bear (see Jer 23:33 and note).

the land of Hadrach A city in northern Syria that is located about 30 miles southwest of Aleppo. Identified with modern Tell Afis. This is the only mention of this place in the OT.

          Hadrach EDB

Damascus A city in southern Syria; the chief city of the Arameans.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 2, 'Hamath A city in northern Syria that is located on the Orontes River about 130 miles north of Damascus.

  This city, which is mentioned many times in extrabiblical documents, was a center of trade due to its strategic location on the major trade routes connecting Africa with Asia Minor. Hamath is to be identified with the modern Hama.

          Hamath EDB

Tyre The most important Phoenician city. See note on Neh 13:16; note on Ezek 26:2.

Sidon A city in central Phoenicia (modern Lebanon). Located about 25 miles south of modern-day Beirut. Jezebel was from Sidon.

          Sidon EDB

they are very wise Likely mocking Tyre and Sidon for their confidence in their own abilities. Ezekiel similarly mocks the ruler of Tyre for his arrogant reliance on his own wisdom (see Ezek 28:3–5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 3, 'a fortification A heavily fortified defensive stronghold.

gold like the mud of the streets Tyre became incredibly wealthy as a center of trade and commerce.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 4, 'will drive it out Tyre’s downfall is depicted with similar imagery in Ezek 26:4–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 5, 'The four cities mentioned here were part of the “Philistine pentapolis” (Gaza, Gath, Ekron, Ashkelon, and Ashdod). See note on Zeph 2:4.
           Biblical Places
           Philistines EDB


        Philistines');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 6, 'the pride of the Philistines Refers to the wealth and power of the nation. Everything the Philistines took pride in would be destroyed when Yahweh came in judgment (compare Isa 13:19; 16:6; Jer 13:9; Hos 5:5; 7:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 7, 'its blood from its mouth Perhaps intimating that the Philistines consumed the blood, something Yahweh had forbidden (Gen 9:4; Lev 3:17). The blood is likely that of animal sacrifices offered to idols.

abominations The Hebrew word here is commonly used to refer to idols, associating them with something disgusting or abhorrent (see 1 Kgs 11:7; 2 Kgs 23:24). Here, it probably refers to meat sacrificed to idols.

          Idolatry LTW

the Jebusite A tribe of unknown origin who were the inhabitants of Jerusalem (Jebus) before David captured the city in 1004 BC.

          Jebus HBD');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 8, 'I will encamp at my temple Refers to the temple in Jerusalem. Yahweh will return to dwell in Jerusalem and protect His people from oppression by foreign nations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 9, 'Zechariah draws on the language and imagery of a number of other OT passages in these two verses. His description of the messianic king echoes passages from Isaiah and Micah (e.g., Isa 9:6–7; 11:1–5; Mic 5:2–4). The extent of the king’s power and influence also contains echoes of Psa 72 (especially Psa 72:1–11). The nations’ acceptance of Judah’s rule and the references to a donkey and a donkey’s colt are likely allusions to Gen 49:10–11.
           Zechariah 9:9–10 WBCV32:MM
           Zechariah 9:9 Zechariah (NAC)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 9, 'O daughter of Zion Refers to the inhabitants of Jerusalem (see note on Isa 1:8).

Your king Refers to the Messiah (compare Jer 23:5).

righteous and victorious Yahweh has equipped the coming messianic king with righteousness (Isa 9:7; 11:4–5; compare Psa 72:1). He will fulfill Yahweh’s ideal for kingship (see note on Jer 22:3; compare Deut 10:18).

a donkey An animal similar to a horse that is used for riding or as a beast of burden. A donkey was a common mount for transportation in biblical times (see Num 22:21; Judg 5:10; 2 Sam 16:2). In this context, the reference to a donkey is likely meant evoke th');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 10, 1, 'the season of the spring rain The rains that commence in March or April are known as the spring rain or latter rain. The former rains are those of autumn that follow the harvest time. See Joel 2:23 and note.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 10, 2, 'household gods The Hebrew term used here (teraphim) is also used for the household idols that Rachel steals from her father, Laban (see Gen 31:19 and note). These idols might have been used for ancestor worship.

          Background Studies: Household Gods LRC:G1250

those who practice divination Those who use a variety of techniques to determine messages from the gods. Such practices are prohibited in biblical law (Deut 18:10–14). The prophets frequently condemn these methods of attempting to communicate with supernatural beings (see Ezek 13:23; 21:21; Jer 27:9; Mic 3:6–7).

see a lie The prophet appears to be condemning those who gave false hope to God’s people with their optimistic messages (compare Jer 14:13–18; 23:16–17; 27:8–11; 28:15; Ezek 13:1–23).

the people wander like sheep The people were described as God’s flock in Zech 9:16. The metaphor of the people as sheep is fairly common in the OT (see Psa 74:1; 95:7; 100:3; Ezek 34:31).

there is no shepherd Without godly leadership, the people go astray and suffer (compare Ezek 34:5 and note). The leaders should be protecting the people as a shepherd would protect his flock, but they have failed (see 1 Kgs 22:17; Jer 2:8; note on Ezek 34:1–10). In Ezekiel, Yahweh Himself takes over for the failed shepherds (Ezek 34:10–11; compare Psa 78:72), but He also assigns that role to His Messiah (Ezek 34:23; 37:24; Mic 5:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 10, 3, 'against the shepherds Compare Ezekiel 34:10.

the leaders The Hebrew term here, literally meaning “the he-goats” or “rams,” is being used metaphorically for those who would lead the flock (compare Isa 14:9; Jer 50:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 10, 4, 'the cornerstone The first stone placed when erecting a building. Each term used in this verse—cornerstone, tent peg or nail, and battle bow—is used metaphorically for the leaders of Israel, who are depicted as great warriors (see Zech 10:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 10, 6, 'the house of Joseph I will save The northern kingdom of Israel is sometimes referred to as Joseph or Ephraim, using the most powerful northern tribe as a representative of the entire kingdom (compare Psa 78:67).

I have compassion on them The prophets frequently indicate that Yahweh’s compassion for His people motivates Him to restore and redeem them (see Isa 14:1; 54:8; Jer 12:15; 13:14; 31:20; 33:26; Ezek 39:25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 10, 7, 'the people of Ephraim Referring to the northern kingdom of Israel. See note on Hos 4:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 10, 8, 'I will whistle to them Reflects a shepherd who whistles for his sheep.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 10, 9, 'Though I sow them among the nations A common image for Israel’s punishment for breaking the covenant: They were taken captive and resettled in different lands. They were scattered, but Yahweh will bring them back (compare Isa 11:11–12; 27:13; Hos 11:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 10, 11, 'sea of This imagery of Yahweh’s subjugation of the waters so His people can return home is likely an allusion (or indirect reference) to Isa 11:15.

The pride of Assyria The condemnation of the “pride” of a nation typically refers to whatever that people prized most as a source of identity and distinctiveness. For Assyrians, it likely refers to their military might. See note on Zech 9:6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 1, 'Chapter 11 paints a gloomy picture of death and destruction. The narrative of the shepherd of the sheep for slaughter in vv. 4–16 is surrounded by two poetic oracles in vv. 1–3 and 11:17. The first describes the destruction of the natural beauty of the regions north of Israel—Lebanon, Bashan, and the fertile Jordan Valley. The closing oracle pronounces woe on the shepherd who has failed in his duty to protect his flock. These three segments are unified by the continued use of shepherd imagery, introduced in 10:2–3. The basic message is one of judgment on the people and on their leaders for rejecting Yahweh. In the central narrative, Yahweh orders the prophet to become shepherd of the “sheep for slaughter.” His service appears to symbolize Yahweh’s relationship with Israel through the prophets: They attempt to lead the people back to Yahweh, but the people persist in their obstinate rebellion until both prophet and Yahweh are frustrated.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 1, 'O Lebanon The mountainous region north of the Sea of Galilee, known for its cedar trees (see note on Isa 14:8). Cedar was valuable as a building material in the Mediterranean world. It also was the largest and most majestic of the region’s trees.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 2, 'O juniper A type of evergreen fir tree known for its thick foliage. Only the cedar trees are destroyed; the cypresses (or junipers, in some translations) and oaks are called to mourn the loss of the glorious cedar forests.

oaks of Bashan Oaks, the other prized tree of the region, often were used as symbols of strength and power. The cedars of Lebanon and oaks of Bashan are similarly paired in Isa 2:13 and Ezek 27:5–6. Amos 2:9 also uses cedar and oak to symbolize height and power, respectively. Bashan is a region of the northern Transjordan known for its fertility (Mic 7:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 3, 'the thickets of the Jordan The largest and most important river in Canaan. Its headwaters originate in the region of Mount Hermon, and the river flows south to the Dead Sea. The vegetation along the banks of the Jordan River is extremely dense, creating a tropical jungle that contrasted sharply with the surrounding barren terrain and provided a habitat for wildlife, including lions. Jeremiah uses similar language to describe the area (see Jer 12:5; 49:19; 50:44).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 4, 'Shepherd The idea of a prophet (Zechariah) as the symbolic representation of his message is common in prophetic literature (e.g., Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel, Hosea, Jonah).

        Symbolic Actions of the Prophets Table

doomed to slaughter Refers to the herd of animals kept outside the temple for sacrificial offering. This image symbolizes that the Israelites are doomed to be conquered by foreign nations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 5, 'Their own shepherds The leaders have taken advantage of the people instead of caring for them (compare Isa 1:23; Amos 5:11–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 6, 'am going to cause humankind to fall, each into the hand of his neighbor A reference to the conquests of the northern kingdom by Assyria and the southern kingdom by Babylon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 7, 'I shepherded Zechariah is now speaking.

two staffs A long stick used by shepherds to defend sheep against wild animals.

one I called Kindness The staffs are indicative of the shepherd’s guiding principles. The name used here can denote beauty, favor, or delight, symbolizing God’s blessing on His people (Psa 90:17).

the other I called Unity The name can denote harmony, unity, and bands or bonds. Harmony or agreement is the outcome of leadership guided by divine favor.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 8, 'three shepherds The identity of these shepherds is unclear. Many possibilities have been suggested, such as the last three kings of Judah: Jehoiakim, Jehoiachin, and Zedekiah.

  It might be preferable to take the three shepherds as symbolic of the ineffective leadership of Israel and Judah as a whole. Three is a common symbolic number that can represent completeness. Their removal after a short period of time could represent divine judgment for their failure.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 9, 'let them devour the flesh of each other A reference to the cannibalism that accompanied the siege of Jerusalem (see Deut 28:54–55; Lam 4:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 10, 'to break my covenant that I had made Israel had broken the terms of Yahweh’s covenant (compare Jer 11:6–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 12, 'give me my wages Having lost his sheep, the prophet asks the nation to provide his wages.

thirty silver shekels The afflicted nation determined that the service of the shepherd was worth 30 pieces of silver, the legal compensation for the loss of a slave (see Exod 21:32). The reference here is to the shekel, the basic unit of silver that weighed just over 11 grams.

  Thirty shekels of silver was a significant sum. Nehemiah 5:15 refers to 40 shekels as a burdensome tax collected by previous governors. Matthew’s Gospel likely alludes to this passage in recounting the 30 pieces of silver given to Judas Iscariot for betraying Jesus (Matt 26:15). Later, Matthew reports the fulfillment of this prophecy when the priests use the 30 pieces of silver discarded by Judas to buy a potter’s field (Matt 27:3–10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 13, 'Throw it to the potter Refers to the potter’s field, a place of debris where a potter');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 1, 'The second revelatory oracle (Zech 12:1–14:21) focuses on the events surrounding Yahweh’s second coming and His enthronement as the King of Israel. The consistent use of the phrase “in that day” demonstrates the future-oriented nature of the oracle.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 1, 'who stretches out the heavens, founds the earth Demonstrates Yahweh’s omnipotence.

forms The term used here is commonly used to describe the work of potters as they mold clay.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 2, 'a cup of reeling Describes a cup of wine that causes the person drinking it to stagger. The surrounding nations will become like a stumbling, drunken man. Isaiah 51:17, 22 and Jeremiah 25:15–16 also use this metaphor (see note on Jer 25:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 3, 'that day Refers to the day that Jerusalem becomes a cup of staggering—the day of God’s judgment.

a stone weight Describes a stone that injures those who try to lift it. Those nations who attempt to attack Jerusalem will find the task too difficult and will end up injured.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 4, 'my eyes God declares that He will watch over the house of Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 6, 'a pan of fire The term used here designates a cooking vessel (1 Sam 2:14) or a basin for water, such as the laver (e.g., Exod 30:18). In this case, the pot is filled with coals that burn the surrounding wood. This imagery alludes back to Zech 11:1–3, where the trees representing kings are burned with fire.

to the right and to the left Since the Israelites orient themselves to the east, the right would be south and the left would be north—the same directions that the chariots of 6:6 went as they traveled out to patrol the earth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 7, 'the tents of Judah A metaphor referring to the towns and villages that surround Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 8, 'one who stumbles among them Under Yahweh’s direct protection, even the weak become strong. Compare Isaiah 60:22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 10, 'grace and supplication Grace is special favor freely given by God to the nation, whereas pleas for mercy represent Yahweh’s response to the nation’s petitions. The two together portray the vast extent of divine providence.

whom they pierced The change in pronoun is done for stylistic reasons; the subject is still God. This phrase refers to the heartbreaking effect the nation’s idolatry has on God (compare Zech 13:3).

  John’s Gospel uses this phrase at the end of a sequence of OT allusions and quotations designed to show how the details of Jesus’ crucifixion aligned with biblical prophecy. In John 19:32–37, the soldiers pierce Jesus’ side with a spear, and John identifies that action as the fulfillment of Zech 12:10 (see John 19:37).

          John 19:37 CNTUOT

one wails over an only child The great mourning will resemble the laments that accompanying the loss of an only child.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 11, 'Hadad Rimmon The name of the Aramean storm god. However, the term here seems to identify a location near Megiddo. While this location is unknown, the mourning referenced here was for the death of Josiah in 609 BC (2 Chr 35:22–25). Josiah was the last godly king of Judah.

          Hadad-Rimmon EDB

        Pagan Deities in the Old Testament Table



the plain of Megiddo The Plain of Esdraelon was dominated by the powerful fortress city of Megiddo.

          Biblical Places

          Megiddo HBD');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 12, 'the house of David Refers to the royal family.

the house of Nathan The third son of David born in Jerusalem. Shortly after the exile, the royal line began to be traced through Nathan. (Zerubbabel was of the line of Nathan, not Solomon.)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 13, 'the Shimeites The family of Shimei, the grandson of Levi (Num 3:18, 21). Here they represent the priestly line in Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 14, 'all those clans Everyone will mourn, from the least to the greatest.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 13, 1, 'that day The Day of Yahweh was a day of judgment (Zech 12:2–3) as well as a day of restoration.

the house of David and for the inhabitants of Jerusalem Representative of the whole redeemed people.

cleanse them from sin and from impurity The people must be restored to a state of purity before God. Washing with water was one of the methods for restoring cleanness outlined in biblical law (e.g., Lev 13–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 13, 2, 'I will cut off the names of the Refers to removing the idols completely so that not even the memory of them remains.

the prophets Refers to the false prophets.

the spirit of uncleanness A spirit that dwells in the false prophets (1 Kgs 22:19–23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 13, 3, 'You shall not live Deuteronomy 13:1–11 commands the death penalty for those who engage in idolatry and false prophecy. In this restored future, loyalty to Yahweh trumps loyalty to family.

a lie in the name of Yahweh Falsely claiming to speak for Yahweh also merited the death penalty (see Deut 18:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 13, 4, 'they will not put on The false prophet’s shame is demonstrated by his refusal to wear a prophet’s uniform and his denial of being a prophet (Zech 13:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 13, 5, 'I am not a prophet False prophets will go into hiding and masquerade as farmers. The false prophets’ claim echoes Amos 7:14, where Amos asserts that he is not a professional prophet—just a working man called by God. By contrast, the false prophet is speaking the truth that he is no prophet in actuality; God has not called him, but he is hiding his past—that he once claimed to speak for God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 13, 6, 'these wounds between your arms False prophets often cut themselves as part of their idolatrous rituals. The custom originated as a way to show one’s devotion to the gods.

  The prophets of Baal cut themselves in an attempt to encourage Baal to send fire from heaven (see 1 Kgs 18:28). The law forbade the Israelites from cutting themselves, though the specific reference is to the practice of cutting oneself to identify with the dead (see Lev 19:28; Deut 14:1).

in the house of the ones who love me Possibly a reference to a pagan temple.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 13, 7, 'my shepherd Here the metaphor of the shepherd likely represents a royal leader, using the imagery in the sense of Ezekiel and Jeremiah (see Ezek 34:1–10; Jer 23:1–6; compare 1 Kgs 22:17). The shepherd imagery of Zech 10–11 likely included religious leaders such as false prophets (see 11:16–17).

Strike the shepherd The NT quotes this verse in relation to the arrest of Jesus (see Matt 26:31; Mark 14:27).

the sheep may be scattered A common consequence of removing the leader (compare 1 Kgs 22:17; Ezek 34:5–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 13, 9, 'into the fire Possibly a reference to going into exile.

They are my people A covenant formula expressing the intimate relationship between Yahweh and Israel (see Exod 6:7; Lev 26:12). Hosea uses this formulaic language to describe breaking as well as restoring the relationship (Hos 1:9; 2:23). Variations on the formula appear a number of times in Jeremiah and Ezekiel (e.g., Jer 7:23; 11:4; 24:7; 31:33; Ezek 36:28; 37:27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 1, 'The events described in Zech 14:1–2 correspond to Joel 2:1–11. Jeremiah refers to this period as a time of distress for Jacob (Jer 30:7), whereas Daniel refers to it as the most distressing time since the beginning of nations (Dan 12:1). Each of these passages describes a period of great suffering for the nation of Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 1, 'A day is coming for Yahweh A phrase used throughout the prophets to denote the day, when God will intervene in human affairs and judge the wicked (Isa 13:6, 9; Ezek 30:3; Joel 1:15; 2:1; Amos 5:18; Obad 15; Mal 4:5) or bless the righteous (Joel 3:16–21; Obad 17–21; Zeph 3:9–20).

          Day of the Lord HIBD

will be divided in your midst Refers to a conquering army dividing the spoils of war.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 2, 'I will gather all the nations Yahweh gathers the nations in order to purify His people (Zech 13:9) and judge their enemies (Joel 3:2; Zech 14:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 3, 'Yahweh will go forth The depiction of Yahweh as a warrior is a common motif in the OT.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 4, 'the Mount of Olives An extended ridgeline bordering Jerusalem to the east. The Mount of Olives is higher in elevation than Jerusalem; it is situated 230 feet above the temple mount.

          Olives, Mount Of HBD

by a very great valley Refers to the Valley of Jehoshaphat—the valley of Yahweh’s judgment (compare Joel 3:2). This great valley will allow the faithful remnant to escape the armies that have captured Jerusalem (Zech 14:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 5, 'the days of King Uzziah of Judah Also known as Azariah, meaning “Yahweh is my strength”; reigned ca. 792–742 BC. God blessed Uzziah with a long and prosperous reign because he did right in His sight (2 Kgs 15:3). See note on 2 Chr 26:1.

          Uzziah EDB');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 8, 'living waters Describes waters flowing rapidly with constant movement and a variety of twists and turns, so that they appear to be something alive. Ezekiel 47:1–12 also describes a river flowing from Jerusalem in the last days.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 9, 'Yahweh will be one An allusion drawing attention to the statement of Yahweh’s essential unity in Deut 6:4 (sometimes referred to as the Shema).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 10, 'into a desert plateau The rugged terrain south of Jerusalem that is filled with steep hills and deep valleys. It is commonly called the Judaean Wilderness.

from Geba A town given to the Levites located about six miles north of Jerusalem in the territory of Benjamin. The town is to be identified with the modern Jaba.

Rimmon south of Jerusalem A town originally given to the tribe of Simeon located about nine miles north of Beer-sheba. The town is commonly identified with the modern Horvat Rimmon. The name of the town may be related to the Aramean storm god Rimmon.

in its place Each of the subsequent locations is within Jerusalem.

the Gate of Benjamin See note on Jer 20:2. Probably to be identified with the Sheep Gate (see note on Neh 3:1).

the former gate Located on the northern part of the city, possibly the Fish Gate; see note on Neh 3:3.

the Corner Gate Possibly the Valley Gate, which is located near the southwest corner of the city and opens into the Hinnom Valley.

from the Tower of Hananel A tower on the northern side of the city near the temple (Neh 2:8).

the king’s winepresses Probably refers to the king’s garden of Neh 3:15 that is located near the Pool of Siloam at the southeastern corner of the city.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 12, 'the plague Alludes to the plagues Yahweh brought against Egypt in Exod 7–11 (compare Zech 14:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 14, 'the wealth of all the surrounding nations A reversal of the situation of v. 1, where Jerusalem was plundered.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 16, 'every survivor The prophets describe how all the nations will one day come to worship Yahweh (compare Isa 2:3; Mic 4:2; Zech 8:20).

the Feast of Booths One of the three feasts during which every male Jew was required to be in Jerusalem (Deut 16:16).

  During this festival, the Jews commemorated the wilderness wanderings of the exodus generation by living in tents set up around Jerusalem. The dedication of Solomon’s temple took place during this feast.

          Feasts HBD');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 18, 'the clan of Egypt All the nations of the world, including Egypt, will worship Yahweh (see Isa 19:21 and note). They will be subject to judgment for failing to observe');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 0, 0, '1:1 This verse acts as a title or heading to the book of Malachi, which addresses the people of Judah sometime between the completion of the rebuilt temple (515 BC) and the period of Nehemiah (ca. 444–432 BC). The exiles had returned from Babylon, but the people’s faithfulness to Yahweh and His covenant continued to waver.
   Malachi’s messages appear to indicate that while the prophets Haggai and Zechariah had motivated the people to finish the temple and reinstitute the temple service, their efforts to renew the people’s commitment to Yahweh had not resulted in long-term change (these efforts began in 520 BC; Ezra 5:1). It is for this reason that Ezra the priest’s actions were required (beginning in 458 BC and continuing into the time of Nehemiah; Ezra 7–10). There are similarities between the message of Malachi and the reforms of Ezra and Nehemiah, which could indicate that Malachi is their contemporary (compare Mal 3:7–10 to Neh 10:37–39; also compare Mal 2:10–16 to Neh 13:23–29 and Ezra 9–10).


        Introduction to Malachi

        The Minor Prophets

        How to Study the Bible



An oracle See note on Nah 1:1.

        Prophetic Commissioning and the Divine Presence

to Israel The remnant of people who returned from exile in Babylon. This title is used because Israel serves as another name for the people’s forefather Jacob (Gen 35:10), whose election is described in the verses following his naming.

  In 538 BC, the Persian Empire—who had succeeded Babylon as the world’s superpower—allowed for the Jewish people to return from their exile to their homeland. The words of Malachi were addressed to this group of people, who were now living in Jerusalem.

Malachi Meaning “my messenger.” Possibly a descriptive title of the author rather than a personal name.

  The argument that “malachi” is a title rather than a name is based on the following reasons: the name appears nowhere else in the OT; the name is highly unusual in that it ends with the possessive i (meaning “my”); the Greek translation (Septuagint) and the Aramaic translation (Targum) took the phrase to represent an office as opposed to a proper name; and the book contains no information regarding the prophet’s parentage or hometown. Since the phrase is an anonymous designation in Mal 3:1, it ought to be the same in v. 1. In addition, there is no mention of Malachi in Ezra and Nehemiah, whereas the prophets Haggai and Zechariah are both mentioned in Ezra 5:1.

  The similarity of Mal 1:1 with Zech 9:1 and 12:1, two other allegedly anonymous prophecies, may indicate that these two prophecies in Zechariah and the book of Malachi came from the same anonymous prophet. The book of Malachi may have been separated from the others to result in 12 Minor Prophets, a highly symbolic number—perhaps representing the 12 tribes of Israel).

    

          Symbolic Names of People in Hebrew Table

          Malachi, Book of EDB



 1:2–5 Malachi is written in a disputation style, much like the record of a court case. The pattern is used to raise a point made by certain individuals, and then offer a contradiction in a point-counterpoint formula. There are six cycles of disputation in Malachi. Each cycle begins with Yahweh making a statement of truth concerning His character. A hypothetical audience then offers a rebuttal in the form of a question. Each cycle ends with Yahweh responding by presenting supporting evidence. Other examples of the disputation pattern include Isa 40:27–28; Jer 2:23–37; Ezek 12:21–28; and Mic 2:6–11.


1:2 I have loved you This Hebrew term for love is a technical term in ancient Near Eastern treaty and covenant texts indicating choice or election to covenant relationship. Here, the term speaks of Yahweh’s confirmation of His covenant with Abraham to Jacob rather than Esau (see Gen 28:13–15; 35:9–12). Normally, the oldest son would have been favored.

Esau The son of Isaac and older brother of Jacob (see Gen 25:21–26). Esau’s descendants were the inhabitants of Edom, the region to the southeast of Judah on the southeastern corner of the Dead Sea. The area is arid and mountainous. Edom was perennially in conflict with Israel and Judah. This discordant relationship begins with the sibling rivalry between Esau and Jacob, the forefather of Judah. See Romans 9:13 and note.

          Esau DOT: P

          Esau HBD

Jacob The son of Isaac and younger brother of Esau (see Gen 25:21–26).

          Jacob DOT: P

          Jacob HBD

1:3 Esau I have hated The term for hate is an ancient Near Eastern covenant term. It is used here to denote rejection. Yahweh chose Jacob, not Esau, to continue the covenant relationship began with Abraham. This choice reflects a regular pattern in the OT where a younger son is favored (e.g., Abel in Gen 4, Isaac in Gen 21, Joseph in Gen 37, and David in 1 Sam 16). This recurring theme reveals that Yahweh is not bound by the conventions of human society. He is not obligated to choose someone just because of the priority of their birth.

I have made his mountain ranges a desolation The destruction described here is probably what resulted from the repeated invasions of Edom by the Babylonians between 605 and 540 BC. There is no evidence that Edom was invaded or destroyed during Nebuchadnezzar’s campaign against Judah around 586 BC. However, Nabonidus, one of Nebuchadnezzar’s later successors, claims to have destroyed a place that was probably the Edomite capital city of Bozrah around 552 BC.

          Babylonian Conquest of and Campaigns Against Jerusalem and Judah

the jackals of the desert A canine similar to but smaller than a wolf. These nocturnal scavengers howl with an eerie wail. See note on Lam 4:3.

1:4 I will tear down Yahweh will frustrate all of the Edomites’ attempts to reassert themselves as a regional power. This detailed picture of Yahweh’s attitude toward Edom appears to represent a counterexample to His attitude toward Judah.

  Although Edom was able to rebuild their empire after the Babylonian conquest, they quickly fell prey to the Nabateans (ca. 350 BC; compare Obad 7–9).

1:5 beyond the borders of Israel The territory of Edom is located outside the land given to Israel by Yahweh.



 1:6–2:9 Yahweh rebukes the priests for their failure to maintain the temple service with proper honor and respect. The people should show honor toward God as a son would honor his father, but Israel shows dishonor through their indifference, carelessness, and deception with regard to bringing sacrifices and offerings (Mal 1:6–9). No sacrifice at all would have been better than their polluted offerings (v. 10). The priests were allowing animals with physical defects to be offered as sacrifices even though biblical law stipulated only unblemished animals were appropriate for sacrifice (see Lev 1:3 and note). The priests do not even appear to recognize the seriousness of this violation (Mal 1:13). The priests above all should realize their responsibility to carry out their duties in a way that brings honor to Yahweh. Their failure brings judgment, and they will be held to account for causing the people to stumble (');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 0, 0, '2:2 you will not listen This is the same Hebrew word used in Deut 6:4 (shama). There, however, the emphasis is on hearing with understanding, whereas here it indicates obedience. This parallels the allusion to Deut 6:4 in Mal 2:10. Collectively, these allusions act as a reminder to the people of their commitments to Yahweh.

          Shama WSOTDICT

I will send the curse on you Violating the covenant, like breaking a contract, invoked the negative consequences stipulated by the agreement, specifically curses as described in Lev 26 and Deut 28. Malachi’s use of the rare word me''erah (“curse”) here and in Mal 3:9 undoubtedly alludes to Deut 28:20, one of the only other occurrences of the term in the OT.

your blessings Likely mentioned to evoke memory of the blessings described in Lev 26 and Deut 28.

I have already cursed them The promised blessings for God’s people have not materialized and will not as long as the people continue in their disobedience. The community is already in a state of poverty because of their unfaithfulness (compare Hag 1:10–11).

2:3 I am going to rebuke your offspring Refers to judgment, but the Hebrew phrasing to rebuke (go''er) the offspring (zera'') is unusual. Possibly zera'' is meant as seed for planting, making the rebuke about preventing a good harvest (Lev 26:20). However, a more serious curse directed at the priests would be the loss of family (Deut 28:18, 41). Cutting off someone’s offspring (zera'') refers to bringing their family line to an end (1 Sam 24:21; Psa 37:28).

  Cutting off something is a more common idiom of judgment (compare Mal 2:12; Lev 7:20). If this idiom was meant here, the traditional Hebrew text may have been corrupted from the visually similar verb god''ea. In that case, the Hebrew reading here would be essentially the same as the statement in 1 Sam 2:31 about cutting off the arm (gada'' zeroa; the arm symbolized strength). Ancient Hebrew was written without vowels, making the words for arm and offspring look identical. The ancient Greek translator of this verse in the Septuagint also appears to have read the verse this way, translating “I am separating your shoulder” (Mal 2:3 LXX).

I will scatter offal on your faces The priests will be carried away outside the camp along with the refuse from the sacrifices that must be disposed of (Exod 29:14; Lev 4:11–12).

2:4 my covenant with Levi continues Yahweh had made a special covenant with the tribe of Levi, commanding them to teach His laws to the rest of the nation (see Lev 10:11; Deut 31:11–13; 33:10). Yahweh’s attitude toward His covenant is consistent; His rebuke is meant for correction and does not symbolize that He has abandoned His people (compare Prov 3:11–12).

          Discipline LTW

2:5 with him Levi was the ancestor of Aaron who was ordained as the first priest of Israel (see Exod 28–29). Levi did not personally serve as a priest and is not prominent in Genesis. (He is primarily mentioned in passing in lists of Jacob’s sons apart from the story in Gen 34:1–31). In Mal 2:5–6, he stands symbolically for the institution of the priesthood in general.

2:6 The instruction of truth was in his mouth The things taught by the priests were accurate, at least initially. “His mouth” refers collectively to the Levitical priesthood.

2:7 the messenger of The Hebrew word used here, mal''akh, is the common word for a messenger. Since Malachi’s name means “my messenger,” the use of the word here may be a play on the prophet’s name. See note on 1:1.

2:8 from the way The image of following the appropriate way or path is used throughout Proverbs to designate the way of wisdom and righteousness (see Prov 2:8, 20; 4:11; 6:23).

        Wisdom in Proverbs

2:9 are favoring your own instruction The Hebrew uses the same idiom for showing favoritism or partiality as in Mal 1:8 (see note on 1:8). Lifting the face (nasa panim) is an idiom for showing favoritism. Here it is used with reference to the law, and amounts to an accusation that the priests are breaking God’s repeated commands to deal fairly with everyone (Exod 23:3; Lev 19:15; Deut 16:19).



 2:10–16 This is the third of six disputes in Malachi between God and His people (see note on Mal 1:2–5). Here, Malachi shifts from speaking on behalf of Yahweh to speaking as one of the people of Israel. In v. 11, Malachi critiques God’s people as a whole. This indicates that the first two disputes proved that Yahweh was in the right, and adequately showed the people’s guilt; now the people must respond by correcting their actions.


2:10 one father Possibly an indirect reference (or allusion) to Isa 63:16. The idea of Israel as the offspring of Yahweh originally appears in Exod 4:22, where the nation is identified as Yahweh’s firstborn (compare Jer 31:9).

Has not one God created us The “we” and “us” here specifically refer to the nation of Israel, not all people.

one God An allusion to Deut 6:4. This is a reminder that the people should serve God with their entire being.

profaning the covenant of Israel had entered into a contract with Yahweh (Exod 24:1–8). Their recent actions broke the terms of that contract and demonstrated their apathy toward those terms.

2:11 has married the daughter of a foreign god May refer to intermarriage with fo');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 0, 0, '3:1 I am going to send my messenger An individual who prepares the way for the arrival of the Messiah (see Isa 40:3–5). Malachi later identifies this messenger as the prophet Elijah (Mal 4:5).

  This messenger is identified in the NT as John the Baptist (Matt 11:10; Mark 1:2), the forerunner of Jesus Christ. Mark quotes this verse juxtaposed with Isa 40:3; both passages are applied to John the Baptist.

          John the Baptist HBD

his temple When the Messiah comes, He will enter the temple in fulfillment of prophecy (see Isa 66:6; Ezek 46:12; Hag 2:9; Zech 6:12).

the Lord whom you are seeking The Jews eagerly anticipated the coming of the promised Messiah and the Day of Yahweh because they understood the coming primarily as a day of salvation for them and judgment on their enemies (compare Amos 5:18–20). However, it would also be a day when they too would be judged for sin (Mal 3:5).

3:2 the day of his coming The Day of Yahweh. See Joel 1:15 and note; Amos 5:18 and note.

a refiner’s fire A refiner uses fire to melt metals such as gold and silver to remove impurities. Fire is both purifying and destructive. Compare Zech 13:9.

launderers’ alkali A fuller or launderer washes cloth to remove oil and grime so that it can be dyed. Both processes described in this verse—refining metal and cleaning clothes—involved separating what was of value from that which was of no value.

3:3 he will purify the children of Levi The sons of Levi were in need of purification because of their disobedience to Yahweh’s covenant (see Mal 2:8).

3:4 will be pleasing Jeremiah 6:20 uses this Hebrew term together with a negative word to describe the unclean sacrifices of his day as not being pleasing to God.

3:5 a swift witness Yahweh will testify against those who are unrighteous. Lists explicitly detailing the people’s offenses are common in the prophets (compare Isa 1:23; Jer 7:5–10; Ezek 22:7–13; Zech 7:10). Certain practices are regularly singled out for criticism—mistreating marginal groups in society such as the poor, orphans, widows, and foreigners; engaging in sexual immorality; and participating in idol worship.

          Adultery LTW

          Idolatry LTW

          Protected Classes LTW



 3:6–12 This fifth disputation continues the focus on Israel’s failure to observe Yahweh’s laws as they had promised (Exod 24:1–8). The first verse asserting that Yahweh Himself has not changed can also be read as another response to the people’s doubts about God’s justice (Mal 2:17), but it also emphasizes the call to repentance in v. 7. The people were the ones who changed and turned away from God, but they question whether it is even possible for them to turn back. Yahweh asserts that one way they could show renewed loyalty would be to stop robbing Him of the tithes and offerings that are rightly His (vv. 8–10). Obedience in this matter would be a significant step in restoring their relationship with God (vv. 11–12).


3:6 I, Yahweh, have not changed The primary reason Israel has not been destroyed is because of Yahweh’s faithfulness to His covenants with the nation. Yahweh will not change His mind concerning Israel.

3:7 Return to me The Hebrew word for returning or turning around appears here with the idea of repentance—one of its common uses.

          Repentance LTW

          Shub WSOTDICT

        Repentance

3:8 the tithes A 10th part of the produce of the ground and livestock. See Lev 27:30 and note.

          Tithe EDB

contributions Freewill offerings. See 1 Chr 29:6.

3:10 the storehouse There were several storehouses located in the temple complex.

I will not open for you the windows of heaven A figure of speech for rain, resulting in agricultural success. The point of this passage is not that Yahweh would bless the people based on their giving, but instead that He would honor His covenant with them, as told in the law (Lev 26:1–14; Deut 28:12). The people were currently experiencing only the negative side of the covenant, because they were not in proper relationship with Yahweh (Mal 3:6–7; see Deut 28:15–68).

  The act of giving was a step toward restoring the people’s relationship with Yahweh. The tithe represents the people’s obedience to their covenant with Yahweh. God is challenging Israel to keep their part of the covenant, so that He may show that He will keep His end of the covenant.

  Paul explains this concept further in 2 Cor 9:5, emphasizing that giving must be done with a cheerful spirit. The gift does not earn God’s favor; instead, it is the natural response of a person in relationship with God.

3:11 the devourer An animal that feeds on agricultural produce, such as a locust.



 3:13–4:3 This passage contains the sixth and final disputation and returns to topics raised in the fourth dispute (Mal 2:17–3:5), especially the perceived futility of obedience and the nature of the Day of Yahweh. However, the day of judgment described here emphasizes the positive experience for those who are faithful to Yahweh (4:1–3). In this dispute, the people give clear expression to the objection that serving Yahweh properly, as the prophet has urged, seems pointless, an objection implied by 2:17 (see note on 2:17–3:5). They repeat the charge that those who practice evil appear to escape judgment, so there is no incentive for them to obey (vv. 13–15). The section in vv. 16–18 answers the charge by emphasizing the existence of those who remained faithful to Yahweh and the reality of the distinction between the righteous and the wicked. The acknowledgment that there would be faithful followers of God likely explains why the discussion of the Day of Yahweh draws attention to the salvation of the righteous (4:1–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Faithlife Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 0, 0, '4:1 The day The Day of Yahweh. Compare Mal 3:2. Compare Joel 1:15 and note; Amos 5:18 and note.

an oven Refers to either an oven used to bake bread or a kiln used to harden pottery.

4:2 the sun of righteousness Although this may be a messianic title, the expression more likely characterizes the day as one of blessing upon the righteous.

4:3 You will trample down the wicked As treading grapes in a winepress.





 4:4–6 These final verses of Malachi are generally understood as the conclusion to the entire book, but it may also function as the conclusion to the sixth disputation. These three verses summarize the essence of Malachi’s message: the need for proper observance of God’s law, the reality of future judgment, and the importance of maintaining a proper society that remains aligned with God’s instruction.


4:4 Remember The ancient Greek translation of the Bible (the Septuagint) places Mal 4:4 after v. 6, an arrangement designed to end the book on a positive note. It also serves to end the book with an explicit call to remain obedient to the law of Moses.

4:5 Elijah the prophet The messenger announced in 3:1 is now explicitly identified as the prophet Elijah (see note on 3:1).

  Second Temple Judaism developed many traditions around the biblical character of Elijah. Since Elijah did not die but was supernaturally taken to heaven (2 Kgs 2:11–12), the expectation of his return played into messianic speculation in the time of Christ. Additionally, it influenced later Jewish traditions that depicted Elijah as an intermediary between the rabbis and Yahweh (see John 1:21 and note). Elijah also appears in the NT at Jesus’ transfiguration (Matt 17:3–4). In rabbinic literature, Elijah appears as a teacher and dialogue partner for the rabbis (as in the Babylonian Talmud tractate Berakhot 3a).

          Elijah DDD

          Elijah EDB

4:6 he will bring back the hearts of the fathers to the sons The Hebrew word for turning or returning (compare Mal 3:7 and note) appears here with the idea of repentance.

        Repentance

with a ban The Hebrew word used here, cherem, is used throughout the first five books of the Bible (the Pentateuch) to refer to the complete destruction of something that has been put under a sacred ban, usually in reference to the Canaanites and their religion (see Exod 22:20 and note; Num 21:2–3; Deut 7:2, 26).

          Cherem WSOTDICT






Matthew



 1:1–17 The Gospel of Matthew probably was written sometime between AD 65–80 by the Apostle Matthew, one of Jesus’ 12 disciples (Matt 9:9; 10:3).
   The book begins with Jesus’ genealogy, identifying Him as a descendant of David and Abraham. These connections serve to establish Jesus’ identity as a Jew (son of Abraham) and His right to rule (son of David). Additionally, both titles evoke messianic expectations and recall God’s promises in His covenants with Abraham and David.
   Matthew arranges his material in three groups of roughly 14 generations each. This may reflect the value of David’s name in Hebrew (14 according to gematria, the ancient practice of assigning numerical values to letters). Matthew traces Jesus’ lineage back to Abraham, though the genealogy is not exhaustive. It gives priority to the theme of the royal messiah rather than to strict chronology.
     

        Introduction to Matthew

        The Synoptic Gospels and Acts

        How to Study the Bible



1:1 of the genealogy Family descent was very important to Matthew’s original audience, who hoped in the promises that God had made to specific ancestors. The book of Matthew shows how Jesus fulfills these promises.

          Genealogy DJG

          Genealogy, Genealogies AYBD

          Gematria JPSGJT

Christ The Greek word used here, christos, can be rendered as “Christ,” “Messiah,” or “anointed one.” This term is used in the OT primarily to describe kings—although other figures, such as priests and prophets, are occasionally referred to as anointed. Matthew applies the term to Jesus and connects Him with David, thereby emphasizing Jesus’ kingly role.

  Jews in the first century had varied and multifaceted expectations of the Messiah. Many longed for a political leader like King David who would free them from Roman oppression and restore national independence. Others anticipated a priestly figure who would legitimize the temple worship, which the Hasmonean rulers had taken over.

    

son of David The Jews expected the true Messiah to be from David’s line (2 Sam 7:11–16).

  That later Jewish polemicists did not challenge this ancestral connection speaks to the credibility of the tradition (although there were many descendants of David in first-century Palestine). However, the implications of Jesus’ relation to David were vigorously contested. For Matthew, Jesus’ Davidic heritage is evidence of His messiahship, stemming from passages such as Jer 23:5 and Jer 33:15. By connecting Jesus with David, Matthew asserts that the Davidic covenant reaches its ultimate fulfillment in Jesus (see 2 Sam 7:16 and note).

          Son of David DJG

          David, Root of ISBE

          The Davidic Charter AYBD

          Old Testament Prophecies about Future Davidic Kings AYBD

Abraham The forefather of Israel. God had promised Abraham that he would be the source of blessing for all peoples (Gen 12:3; compare Acts 3:25; Gal 3:8). For Matthew, this promise ultimately is fulfilled in Jesus.

  Like the reference to David, Abraham’s name would have brought to mind covenant promises and messianic expectations. In addition to identifying Jesus as the means of global blessing (see Gen 12:3 and note), the reference to Abraham evokes a messianic prophecy from OT pseudepigrapha. Testament of Levi 8:14b–15 says, “A king shall arise in Judah, and shall establish a new priesthood, after the fashion of the Gentiles to all the Gentiles. And His presence is beloved, as a prophet of the Most High, of the seed of Abraham our father.” As late as the second or third century BC (the supposed date of composition for Testament of Levi), the Messiah was associated with His Jewish forebear, Abraham. Such an understanding may have influenced Matthew’s presentation of Jesus.

          Testament of Levi 8.15 OT Pseud



1:3 Tamar A neglected widow who used deceit in order to get her rights (Gen 38:26).

          Tamar EDB

          Tamar AYBD

          Genealogy, Genealogies AYBD

1:5 Rahab A prostitute who hid Israelite spies because she knew that Yahweh had giv');
